/* FMT 7–8: Sexual offences, infant deaths; general toxicology */
Z("fmt7",0,"f",["Rape (S. 63 BNS, old IPC 375) is penetration of the vagina, mouth, urethra or anus of a woman by the penis, or insertion of any object or body part, or manipulation or oral contact, against her will, without consent, with consent obtained by fear, impersonation, intoxication or unsoundness of mind, or with or without consent when she is under 18; punishment (S. 64) is rigorous imprisonment of at least 10 years; the medical examination of the victim (S. 184 BNSS — within 24 h, with consent, report to police within 7 days) and of the accused (S. 52 BNSS) follows MoHFW 2014 guidelines, documents injuries, collects evidence for DNA and provides treatment, emergency contraception and counselling.","Rape is a crime of violation of consent — the doctor's job is to treat and support the survivor and collect scientific evidence, not to judge her character.",["Seven circumstances (S. 63): against her will; without consent; consent by fear of death/hurt; consent by impersonation of husband; consent when of unsound mind/intoxicated; with or without consent if < 18; when unable to communicate consent; Exception — medical procedures; sexual acts by husband with wife ≥ 18 (marital exception).","Victim examination: informed consent (≥ 12 yr — own; < 12 — guardian), female attendant, any RMP (government hospital preferred; can't refuse — S. 397 BNSS free treatment), history, general examination (injuries — bite marks, nail marks, bruises on breasts, thighs, wrists), genital examination (vulva, hymen — tears, vagina, perineum; two-finger test banned — Lillu v State of Haryana 2013, Supreme Court 2022), samples — swabs (vaginal, cervical, anal, oral), smears for sperm, clothes, pubic hair combing, nail scrapings, blood/urine for drugs/alcohol, DNA reference; absence of injuries doesn't negate rape.","Accused examination (S. 52 BNSS): with police request (reasonable force), potency/capability, injuries (scratches, bites by victim), smegma (absence suggests recent intercourse), penile swab for vaginal cells/blood, pubic hair, clothes, semen, blood for DNA, alcohol/drugs.","Management: treatment of injuries, emergency contraception (levonorgestrel within 72–120 h), STI prophylaxis, HIV PEP, tetanus, pregnancy test, psychological support (one-stop centres — Sakhi), MTP if pregnant; confidentiality (identity — S. 72 BNS); opinion — 'signs of recent sexual activity/use of force' not 'rape' (legal conclusion)."],"Two-finger test banned; report within 7 days (S. 184 BNSS); doctor does not opine 'rape'."],
"Rape — legal definition (Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, Section 63, corresponding to IPC Section 375 as amended by Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013): a man is said to commit rape if he — (a) penetrates his penis, to any extent, into the vagina, mouth, urethra or anus of a woman or makes her to do so with him or any other person; or (b) inserts, to any extent, any object or a part of the body, not being the penis, into the vagina, urethra or anus of a woman or makes her to do so; or (c) manipulates any part of the body of a woman so as to cause penetration into the vagina, urethra, anus or any part of body of such woman or makes her to do so; or (d) applies his mouth to the vagina, anus, urethra of a woman or makes her to do so — under any of the following seven circumstances: (i) against her will; (ii) without her consent; (iii) with her consent, when her consent has been obtained by putting her or any person in whom she is interested in fear of death or of hurt; (iv) with her consent, when the man knows that he is not her husband and that her consent is given because she believes that he is another man to whom she is or believes herself to be lawfully married; (v) with her consent when, at the time of giving such consent, by reason of mental illness or intoxication or the administration by him personally or through another of any stupefying or unwholesome substance, she is unable to understand the nature and consequences of that to which she gives consent; (vi) with or without her consent, when she is under eighteen years of age; (vii) when she is unable to communicate consent; 'consent' means an unequivocal voluntary agreement communicated by words, gestures or any form of communication; a woman who does not physically resist shall not by reason only of that fact be regarded as consenting; Exception 1 — a medical procedure or intervention shall not constitute rape; Exception 2 — sexual intercourse or sexual acts by a man with his own wife, the wife not being under eighteen years of age, is not rape (marital rape exception — under challenge). Punishments: S. 64 BNS — rigorous imprisonment not less than 10 years, which may extend to imprisonment for life, and fine; aggravated rape (by police officer, public servant, armed forces, jail/hospital staff, relative, guardian, teacher, person in position of trust, on a pregnant woman, on a woman incapable of giving consent, causing grievous harm, repeated rape) — not less than 10 years extending to life (remainder of natural life); S. 65 — rape of a woman under 16 years — not less than 20 years to life; under 12 years — not less than 20 years to life or death; S. 66 — rape causing death or persistent vegetative state — not less than 20 years to life or death; S. 67 — sexual intercourse by husband upon wife during separation; S. 68 — sexual intercourse by a person in authority; S. 69 — sexual intercourse by employing deceitful means (false promise of marriage etc.) — up to 10 years; S. 70 — gang rape (S. 70(1) — not less than 20 years to life; gang rape of a woman under 18 — life or death); S. 71 — repeat offenders — life or death; S. 72 — disclosure of the identity of the victim — punishable; POCSO Act 2012 for victims under 18 (gender-neutral). Medical examination of the victim (survivor): legal provisions — S. 184 BNSS (S. 164A CrPC): when during investigation it is proposed to get the person of the woman (with whom rape is alleged) examined by a medical expert, such examination shall be conducted by a registered medical practitioner employed in a hospital run by the Government or a local authority, and in the absence of such a practitioner, by any other registered medical practitioner, with the consent of such woman or of a person competent to give consent on her behalf, and such woman shall be sent to such RMP within 24 hours from the time of receiving the information; the RMP shall examine her without delay and prepare a report giving name, address, person by whom she was brought, age, description of material taken for DNA profiling, marks of injury, general mental condition, other material particulars, with reasons for each conclusion; the report shall mention that consent was obtained; exact time of commencement and completion of examination noted; the RMP shall forward the report within 7 days to the investigating officer (BNSS; CrPC — without delay); S. 397 BNSS (357C CrPC) — all hospitals, public or private, shall immediately provide first aid or medical treatment free of cost to victims of rape (and acid attack) and inform the police; refusal is punishable (S. 200 BNS / 166B IPC); a female victim may be examined by a male doctor in the presence of a female attendant (preference for female doctor — but no delay); no requisition from police is needed for examination and treatment if the survivor reports directly (she may choose not to report to police — but doctor must inform police as per law, and must respect her choice regarding evidence collection; MoHFW 2014 Guidelines and Protocols — Medico-legal care for survivors/victims of sexual violence). Procedure (MoHFW 2014): (1) informed consent — for examination, sample collection, treatment and informing police (separate consents); consent of survivor herself if ≥ 12 years; parent/guardian for < 12 years; she may refuse any part (refusal documented; treatment still given); (2) history — date, time, place, number of assailants, nature of sexual acts (penetration — vaginal/oral/anal, ejaculation, condom use), use of force, threats, weapons, drugs/alcohol, post-assault activities (bathing, urination, changing clothes, douching — affect evidence), menstrual and obstetric history, last consensual intercourse (for DNA comparison), emotional state; recorded in her words; (3) general physical examination — demeanour, vital signs, signs of intoxication, height, weight, secondary sex characters (age estimation if needed — X-ray, dental, with consent), clothing (tears, stains — collected), injuries on the body — bruises (finger marks on arms, wrists, neck, inner thighs), abrasions, nail scratches, bite marks (swab for saliva DNA before cleaning, photograph with scale), injuries on breasts, buttocks, back (from lying on rough surface), defence injuries; body evidence — foreign material, dried semen (UV light), blood stains; (4) genital and anal examination — external genitalia (labia majora/minora, fourchette — tears, bruises, abrasions, swelling, redness, bleeding, discharge), hymen (note fresh tears — bleeding, oedema, tenderness — or old healed tears; but the status of hymen is irrelevant to the definition of rape and comments on 'habituation to sexual intercourse' or past sexual history must not be made), vagina (speculum examination only when necessary — injuries, foreign bodies, bleeding), cervix, perineum, anus (fissures, tears, laxity, bruising); the 'two-finger test' (per vaginal examination to comment on laxity of vagina/hymen) is unscientific, violates privacy and dignity, and has been banned (Lillu @ Rajesh v. State of Haryana, Supreme Court 2013; MoHFW guidelines 2014; State of Jharkhand v. Shailendra Kumar Rai, Supreme Court 2022 — conducting it is misconduct); per vaginal examination only for medical (treatment) indications; (5) collection of evidence (samples) — clothing worn at the time (dried, packed separately in paper bags); swabs from the body (bite marks, stains, breasts); oral swabs (if oral penetration) and gum line swabs; nail clippings/scrapings (assailant's skin/blood); pubic hair combings (foreign hair) and cut pubic hair (control); vulval swab, vaginal swabs (posterior fornix — high vaginal), cervical swab — two each (one for spermatozoa/smear — dried and labelled; one for semen/DNA), vaginal washing if needed; anal/rectal swabs; blood (for blood group, DNA reference, alcohol and drugs — in grey-top vial with sodium fluoride; HIV, HBV, syphilis serology), urine (pregnancy test, drugs — date-rape drugs like benzodiazepines/flunitrazepam, GHB, ketamine — within 72–96 h); samples air-dried, labelled, sealed and handed to police with chain of custody; time limits — evidence collection most useful within 72 hours (up to 96 h–7 days for some samples); (6) investigations — pregnancy test, STI screening, X-rays for age; (7) treatment — injuries (surgical repair of genital tears), emergency contraception (levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 h, up to 120 h; or copper IUD within 5 days), prophylaxis for STIs (ceftriaxone/azithromycin/metronidazole), HIV post-exposure prophylaxis (within 72 h, 28 days), hepatitis B vaccination, tetanus toxoid, psychological first aid and counselling, referral (One Stop Centres — Sakhi), MTP if pregnancy results (MTP Act — up to 24 weeks for rape survivors; beyond on medical board); follow-up; (8) report and opinion — the doctor documents findings and gives an opinion on (a) whether there are signs of recent sexual activity/penetration (findings consistent with history), (b) whether there are signs of use of force/violence, (c) whether she was under the influence of drugs/alcohol (pending lab), (d) age, (e) samples sent for analysis — final opinion reserved till FSL reports; the doctor must not use the word 'rape' (a legal conclusion for the court), must not comment on character or previous sexual experience, and must state that absence of injuries does not rule out sexual assault (many survivors — especially those who are sexually active, threatened, drugged or children — have no injuries). Medical examination of the accused (S. 52 BNSS / 53A CrPC): when a person is arrested on a charge of rape or attempt and there are reasonable grounds for believing that an examination will afford evidence, it shall be lawful for an RMP employed in a government hospital (or any RMP within 16 km if not available), acting at the request of a police officer (not below sub-inspector), to examine the person, and to use such force as is reasonably necessary; the report includes name, address, age, injuries, description of material taken for DNA profiling and other particulars with reasons; forwarded to investigating officer without delay; examination includes: (1) general — age, build, mental condition, intoxication (smell of alcohol, drugs — blood/urine), identification marks; (2) capability (potency) to perform sexual intercourse — development of external genitalia, secondary sex characters, absence of any disease or deformity which would prevent erection/intercourse (opinion: 'there is nothing to suggest that he is incapable of performing sexual intercourse' — impotence is rarely total; erectile testing not routinely done); (3) signs of recent sexual intercourse — absence of smegma under the prepuce (smegma takes about 24 h to accumulate — its absence in an uncircumcised man suggests intercourse/washing within 24 h), torn frenulum, abrasions on glans/prepuce, female pubic hair, vaginal epithelial cells or blood on the penis (penile swab — coronal sulcus — for victim's DNA; Lugol's iodine test for glycogenated vaginal cells — historical), menstrual blood; semen stains on clothes; (4) signs of struggle — scratches, nail marks, bite marks on face, neck, chest, arms, back, genitals (inflicted by the victim) — photographed and swabbed; (5) samples — clothes, pubic hair (combings and cut), penile swab, blood (DNA reference, grouping, alcohol/drugs), nail clippings, semen (if needed); (6) STI examination (if victim infected); (7) report with opinion. Medicolegal points: corroboration of victim's statement not essential for conviction (sole testimony may suffice); delay in FIR not fatal; medical evidence is corroborative; age determination crucial (< 18 — statutory rape/POCSO); presumption as to absence of consent in certain cases (S. 120 BSA / 114A IEA — if victim states she did not consent, court shall presume); in-camera trial; identity of victim not to be disclosed (S. 72 BNS); compensation (Victim Compensation Scheme); Fast-track courts.",
[["Circumstances of rape (S. 63 BNS)",["Against her will","Without consent","Consent by fear of death/hurt","Consent by impersonation of husband","Consent when of unsound mind/intoxicated","With or without consent if < 18","Unable to communicate consent","Exceptions: medical procedures; husband with wife ≥ 18"]],
["Victim examination — key steps",[["Step","Details"],["Consent","Own (≥ 12 yr); guardian (< 12); separate for exam, samples, police"],["History","Acts, ejaculation, condom, post-assault activities, last consensual sex"],["General exam","Injuries (bruises, bites, nail marks), intoxication"],["Genital/anal exam","Fresh tears, bruises, bleeding; NO two-finger test"],["Samples","Clothes, swabs (vaginal, cervical, oral, anal), nail scrapings, pubic hair, blood/urine"],["Treatment","Injuries, EC, STI prophylaxis, HIV PEP, HBV, counselling"],["Report","Within 7 days to IO (S. 184 BNSS); no 'rape' verdict"]]],
["Accused examination (S. 52 BNSS)",[["Aspect","Findings"],["Potency","Normal genitalia, no disease preventing intercourse"],["Recent intercourse","Absence of smegma, torn frenulum, vaginal cells/blood on penis"],["Struggle","Scratches, bites on face, chest, arms"],["Samples","Penile swab, pubic hair, clothes, blood for DNA, nail clippings"],["Force","Reasonable force permitted on police request"]]],
["Punishments (BNS)",[["Section","Offence","Punishment"],["64","Rape","≥ 10 yr to life"],["65","Victim < 16 / < 12","≥ 20 yr to life / death"],["66","Causing death/PVS","≥ 20 yr to life/death"],["69","Deceitful means","Up to 10 yr"],["70","Gang rape (< 18)","≥ 20 yr / life or death"],["72","Disclosing identity","Punishable"]]]],
{i:"Medical care of a rape survivor combines treatment, dignified evidence collection and impartial documentation within strict legal timelines.",x:[["Landmark rulings",["Lillu v. State of Haryana (2013): two-finger test violates privacy","State of Jharkhand v. Shailendra Kumar Rai (2022): test is misconduct","Absence of injuries does not negate rape","Sole testimony of victim can convict"]]],f:"Survivor reports → free treatment (S. 397 BNSS) → informed consent → history → general + genital examination → samples (chain of custody) → treatment (EC, PEP, STI) → report to IO within 7 days (S. 184 BNSS)\n Accused → police request → S. 52 BNSS examination → potency, recent intercourse, injuries, DNA samples",c:"Rape under S. 63 BNS is non-consensual or under-18 sexual penetration in seven defined circumstances, punishable with at least ten years under S. 64; the survivor must be treated free, examined with consent without the banned two-finger test, have evidence collected and receive emergency contraception, PEP and counselling, with the report sent within seven days, while the accused is examined under S. 52 BNSS for potency, recent intercourse, injuries and DNA samples."});

Z("fmt7",1,"f",["The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act 2012 (amended 2019) is a gender-neutral law protecting all children below 18 years from sexual assault, sexual harassment and pornography; it defines penetrative and aggravated penetrative sexual assault, sexual assault and harassment, mandates reporting by anyone (including doctors) who knows of an offence (S. 19, 21), provides for child-friendly procedures, special courts, medical examination with consent by a female doctor for a girl (S. 27), and stringent punishments including death for aggravated penetrative sexual assault (2019 amendment).","POCSO puts every child under 18 under a protective umbrella — any sexual act with a child is an offence, consent doesn't matter, and every adult who knows must report.",["Offences: penetrative sexual assault (S. 3–4: ≥ 10 yr; < 16 yr victim — ≥ 20 yr), aggravated penetrative (S. 5–6: by police, armed forces, public servant, staff of hospital/school, relatives, gang, < 12 yr, causing injury/pregnancy, repeat — ≥ 20 yr to life or death), sexual assault (touch with sexual intent without penetration — S. 7–8: 3–5 yr), aggravated sexual assault (S. 9–10: 5–7 yr), sexual harassment (S. 11–12: up to 3 yr), using child for pornography (S. 13–15).","Mandatory reporting (S. 19–21): any person including doctor having knowledge must report to Special Juvenile Police Unit (SJPU)/local police; failure — up to 6 months/fine; false complaint (S. 22) punishable.","Medical examination (S. 27; BNSS S. 184): even before FIR; by registered medical practitioner; girl child — by woman doctor; in presence of parent or person child trusts; consent of child/guardian; emergency medical care (Rule 6) without need for FIR or magistrate order — no doctor can demand documentation; free treatment, EC, STI/HIV prophylaxis.","Child-friendly procedures: statement recorded at child's residence/place of choice by woman police officer not in uniform; child not detained at police station overnight; Special Courts (trial within 1 year, in camera, no aggressive questioning, support person, interpreter); identity confidential (S. 23); presumption of guilt (S. 29) and culpable mental state (S. 30); compensation."],"POCSO: < 18 yr, gender-neutral, mandatory reporting (S. 19), girl examined by woman doctor (S. 27)."],
"The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012 (POCSO; in force 14 November 2012; amended 2019; POCSO Rules 2020) was enacted to protect children from offences of sexual assault, sexual harassment and pornography, and to provide for the establishment of Special Courts for trial, with child-friendly mechanisms at every stage of the judicial process; 'child' means any person below the age of 18 years (S. 2(d)); the Act is gender-neutral (victim and offender may be of any gender). Offences and punishments (as amended 2019): (1) penetrative sexual assault (S. 3) — penetration of penis, object or body part into the vagina, mouth, urethra or anus of a child, or making the child do so, or manipulating body parts for penetration, or applying mouth; punishment (S. 4) — imprisonment not less than 10 years, extendable to life, and fine; if the child is below 16 years — not less than 20 years, extendable to life (remainder of natural life); (2) aggravated penetrative sexual assault (S. 5) — when committed by a police officer, member of armed/security forces, public servant, staff of jail, remand home, protection home, observation home, hospital (government or private), educational or religious institution, by a relative or person in a position of trust or authority (guardian, teacher), by a gang, using deadly weapons, fire, corrosive substance, causing grievous hurt or bodily injury to sexual organs, physically incapacitating the child, causing pregnancy, inflicting HIV or life-threatening disease, on a child below 12 years, on a child with mental or physical disability, repeatedly, by a person previously convicted, during communal or sectarian violence, in natural calamity, making the child strip/parade naked, administering drugs/hormones for early sexual maturity; punishment (S. 6) — rigorous imprisonment not less than 20 years, extendable to life (remainder of natural life), and fine, or with death; (3) sexual assault (S. 7) — with sexual intent touching the vagina, penis, anus or breast of the child or making the child touch these of the accused or another, or any other act with sexual intent involving physical contact without penetration; punishment (S. 8) — 3–5 years and fine; (Supreme Court in Attorney General v. Satish, 2021 — skin-to-skin contact not necessary; sexual intent is key); (4) aggravated sexual assault (S. 9) — circumstances as in S. 5; punishment (S. 10) — 5–7 years and fine; (5) sexual harassment (S. 11) — with sexual intent uttering words, making sounds or gestures, exhibiting body parts or objects, making the child exhibit his body, showing pornography, repeatedly following, watching or contacting (directly, electronically), threatening to use body images, enticing; punishment (S. 12) — up to 3 years and fine; (6) using a child for pornographic purposes (S. 13–14) — 5 years (first conviction), 7 years (second) and fine; aggravated forms — 10 years to life; (7) storage of pornographic material involving a child (S. 15) — fine/up to 3–5 years; (8) abetment and attempt (S. 16–18). Procedures and obligations: (A) mandatory reporting (S. 19) — any person (including the child) who has apprehension that an offence is likely to be committed or has knowledge that such an offence has been committed shall provide such information to the Special Juvenile Police Unit (SJPU) or the local police; S. 20 — media, hotel, lodge, hospital, club, studio, photographic facility personnel must report material; S. 21 — failure to report or record a case — imprisonment up to 6 months or fine or both; failure by a person in charge of a company/institution in respect of subordinates — up to 1 year; doctors who examine/treat children with suspected sexual abuse are mandated reporters (confidentiality is overridden); (B) false complaint or false information (S. 22) — punishable (not against a child); (C) media restrictions (S. 23) — no disclosure of identity of child (name, address, photograph, school, family details); (D) recording of statement of the child (S. 24–26) — at the residence of the child or place where he usually resides or place of his choice, as far as practicable by a woman police officer not below the rank of sub-inspector, not in uniform; child not to be detained in police station at night; child not to come in contact with accused; assistance of interpreter, translator, special educator, support person; statement recorded by magistrate (S. 25) — in presence of parents; audio-video recording; (E) medical examination (S. 27): the medical examination of a child in respect of whom any offence has been committed under this Act shall, notwithstanding that a First Information Report or complaint has not been registered, be conducted in accordance with S. 164A CrPC (now S. 184 BNSS); in case the victim is a girl child, the medical examination shall be conducted by a woman doctor; the examination shall be conducted in the presence of the parent of the child or any other person in whom the child reposes trust or confidence; where the parent/trusted person cannot be present, the examination shall be conducted in the presence of a woman nominated by the head of the medical institution; POCSO Rules 2020 (Rule 6) — emergency medical care: where an officer of SJPU or local police receives information, and is satisfied that the child is in need of urgent medical care, he shall arrange to take the child within 24 hours to the nearest hospital/medical care facility; the RMP rendering emergency medical care shall attend to the needs of the child and shall not demand any legal or magisterial requisition or other documentation as a pre-requisite; the emergency care shall include treatment of injuries, emergency contraception (for post-pubertal girls with consent), STI and HIV prophylaxis, referral for mental health counselling; consent of child (if capable) and guardian is needed; forensic evidence collection as per protocols (MoHFW 2014 — child-specific); doctor should use child-friendly language, play aids, avoid re-traumatisation, two-finger test banned; examination findings — often normal (absence of injuries common because of delay, grooming, nature of abuse — fondling, oral contact; rapid healing of genital tissues in children) — normal findings do not exclude abuse; findings suggestive — acute laceration or bruising of hymen/vulva/anus, healed transections of the posterior hymen (between 4 and 8 o'clock), pregnancy, STIs (gonorrhoea, syphilis, HIV, Trichomonas, Chlamydia — beyond perinatal period are highly suggestive), sperm or semen on/in the child's body; (F) Special Courts (S. 28) — Sessions Court designated in each district; trial in camera (S. 37) in presence of parents/trusted person; evidence of child to be recorded within 30 days of taking cognizance, trial completed within 1 year as far as possible (S. 35); child not to be called repeatedly to testify; no aggressive questioning or character assassination; questions through the judge (S. 33); frequent breaks; child-friendly atmosphere; screen between child and accused (S. 36); (G) presumptions — S. 29: where a person is prosecuted for committing or abetting or attempting any offence under S. 3, 5, 7 and 9, the Special Court shall presume that such person has committed the offence unless the contrary is proved (reverse burden); S. 30 — culpable mental state presumed; (H) compensation (S. 33(8), Rule 9) — interim and final compensation for medical treatment and rehabilitation; (I) age determination — as per JJ Act 2015 S. 94 (documents first, then ossification test); (J) offences by children (juvenile offenders) dealt with under JJ Act; (K) consensual adolescent relationships — still an offence under POCSO (age of consent 18), raising debates; courts have shown leniency in some cases. Doctor's role summary: provide emergency care without awaiting police/FIR; report to police (mandatory); examine with consent by a woman doctor for girls in presence of trusted adult; collect evidence; treat (EC, prophylaxis); document carefully; maintain confidentiality of identity; counsel and refer; testify in court.",
[["Offences and punishments (POCSO 2012, amended 2019)",[["Offence","Section","Punishment"],["Penetrative sexual assault","3–4","≥ 10 yr to life; < 16 yr — ≥ 20 yr to life"],["Aggravated penetrative","5–6","≥ 20 yr to life or death"],["Sexual assault (touch with sexual intent)","7–8","3–5 yr"],["Aggravated sexual assault","9–10","5–7 yr"],["Sexual harassment","11–12","Up to 3 yr"],["Child pornography","13–15","5–7 yr; aggravated 10 yr–life"],["Failure to report","21","Up to 6 months/fine"]]],
["Aggravating circumstances (S. 5)",["Police, armed forces, public servant","Staff of hospital, school, jail, home","Relative, guardian, teacher (position of trust)","Gang; deadly weapon; grievous hurt","Child < 12 yr; disabled child","Causing pregnancy, HIV","Repeat offender; communal violence"]],
["Medical examination (S. 27, Rule 6)",["Even without FIR","Girl child — woman doctor","In presence of parent/trusted person","Consent of child/guardian","Emergency care without documentation","EC, STI/HIV prophylaxis, counselling","Two-finger test banned; normal findings don't exclude abuse"]],
["Child-friendly provisions",["Statement at child's place by woman SI not in uniform","No night detention at police station","Special Courts; trial in camera; within 1 year","Evidence within 30 days; no aggressive questioning","Identity confidential (S. 23)","Presumption of guilt (S. 29)","Compensation"]]],
{c:"The gender-neutral POCSO Act protects all children under 18 from sexual assault, harassment and pornography with graded and aggravated offences carrying punishments up to death, mandates reporting by doctors and others, ensures emergency medical care without paperwork and examination of girls by a woman doctor with a trusted adult present, and provides child-friendly investigation, Special Courts and a reverse burden of proof."});

Z("fmt7",2,"f",["Signs of virginity (intact hymen, narrow vagina, firm breasts, intact fourchette) and of defloration (tears of hymen, healed notches, laxity) are unreliable and of very limited medicolegal value, because the hymen may be ruptured by non-sexual causes (sports, masturbation, tampons, injury) or remain intact after intercourse (elastic, fleshy, annular hymen); the 'two-finger test' to assess vaginal laxity is unscientific, violates privacy and dignity, and has been banned by the Supreme Court (2013, 2022) and MoHFW guidelines — a woman's sexual history is irrelevant to rape.","The hymen is not a seal of virginity — it can tear without sex or stay intact after sex, so modern law forbids judging a woman by it.",["Hymen: thin membrane at vaginal orifice; types — annular (commonest), crescentic/semilunar, cribriform, septate, fimbriated, imperforate, vertical, infantile; fresh tear — bleeding, oedema, tenderness, usually posterolateral (5 and 7 o'clock); healing in ~5–10 days; old tear — notches reaching base, carunculae myrtiformes after childbirth.","Intact hymen after intercourse: elastic/fleshy, partial penetration, small penis; ruptured without intercourse: accidents, falls astride, gymnastics, cycling, horse riding, masturbation, tampons, medical examination/instrumentation, ulceration.","Traditional 'true virgin' signs (outdated): intact hymen, narrow vagina with rugae, intact fourchette and posterior commissure, labia majora closely apposed, firm hemispherical breasts — 'false virgin' (hymen intact but sexually active); none conclusive.","Legal: Lillu @ Rajesh v. State of Haryana (2013) — two-finger test violates right to privacy, dignity; MoHFW 2014 guidelines prohibit it and comments on 'habituation'; State of Jharkhand v. Shailendra Kumar Rai (2022) — doctors conducting it guilty of misconduct; S. 53A BSA (old IEA S. 53A, S. 146) — character/previous sexual experience irrelevant to consent; virginity testing condemned by WHO (2018) as human rights violation."],"Hymen status ≠ virginity; two-finger test banned (2013/2022)."],
"Virginity is the state of a female who has not experienced sexual intercourse; defloration is the loss of virginity (rupture of the hymen by sexual intercourse). Historically, forensic textbooks described 'signs of virginity' for medicolegal questions such as nullity of marriage, divorce, defamation, and rape; modern science and law have established that these signs are unreliable and that examination to determine virginity is unscientific and violates human rights. The hymen: a thin fold of mucous membrane partially closing the vaginal orifice, situated at the junction of the vestibule and the vagina; composed of connective tissue with stratified squamous epithelium; its shape, thickness and elasticity vary greatly among individuals and with age and hormonal status (thin and delicate in prepubertal children, thick and elastic under oestrogen after puberty); types — annular (ring-shaped, most common), crescentic/semilunar (posterior), cribriform (multiple openings), septate (two openings), fimbriated (fringed edge — can mimic tears), infantile, vertical, imperforate (no opening — haematocolpos), redundant/elastic/fleshy (may allow intercourse without tearing). Effects of sexual intercourse on the hymen: the first act of intercourse may produce tears (lacerations) of the hymen — usually in the posterior half, at or near the 5 and 7 o'clock positions (or 6 o'clock), extending to the base (attachment to the vaginal wall); fresh tears show bleeding, swelling, redness, tenderness; they heal within 5–10 days, leaving notches/clefts with rounded edges that do not reunite; however, (a) the hymen may remain intact after intercourse when it is elastic, fleshy or loose (dilatable), when penetration is partial (only vulval/vestibular), or with a small penis or gentle intercourse — even pregnancy has occurred with intact hymen; (b) the hymen may be torn or absent without intercourse — accidental injuries (fall astride objects, straddle injuries), vigorous physical activities (gymnastics, horse riding, cycling — though these mostly do not tear it), masturbation, insertion of foreign bodies or tampons, medical examination and instrumentation (speculum, pessaries, D&C), ulceration/disease (diphtheria, noma), congenital absence; after childbirth the hymen is destroyed and remnants appear as small fleshy tags — carunculae myrtiformes (or carunculae hymenales) — a sign of previous vaginal delivery; (c) natural notches and clefts (congenital — usually anterior, symmetrical, not reaching the base) may be mistaken for healed tears. Other 'signs of virginity' described traditionally: labia majora full, firm, closely apposed covering the labia minora; labia minora small, soft, pink; fourchette and posterior commissure intact; fossa navicularis intact; vagina narrow with prominent rugae and reddish walls; breasts firm, rounded, hemispherical with small pink areola and small nipple; perineum intact; these are influenced by age, parity, hormones, race, nutrition and are not reliable. 'True virgin' (intact hymen and other signs, never had intercourse) vs 'false virgin' (hymen intact despite intercourse due to elasticity) — terms of historical interest only. Medicolegal importance and current position: (1) the questions of virginity arose in (a) nullity of marriage (non-consummation — impotence), (b) divorce, (c) defamation (allegations of unchastity), (d) rape cases (traditionally defence used 'previous sexual experience' to discredit the victim), (e) 'virginity testing' of women in customary practices; (2) modern position — WHO, UN Women and OHCHR (2018) called for the elimination of virginity testing, stating it has no scientific merit or clinical indication and is a violation of human rights and a form of violence against women; (3) Indian law — Lillu @ Rajesh v. State of Haryana (Supreme Court, 2013): 'the two-finger test and its interpretation violates the right of rape survivors to privacy, physical and mental integrity and dignity'; the test cannot be used to draw an inference of consent; the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013 inserted S. 53A in the Evidence Act (now S. 49 BSA) — in prosecution for sexual offences, evidence of the character of the victim or of her previous sexual experience with any person shall not be relevant on the issue of consent or the quality of consent; and S. 146 proviso (now S. 151 BSA proviso) — no question in cross-examination about the victim's general immoral character or previous sexual experience; MoHFW Guidelines and Protocols (2014) — 'per-vaginum examination commonly referred to by lay persons as two-finger test must not be conducted for establishing rape/sexual violence and the size of the vaginal introitus has no bearing on a case of sexual violence'; comments such as 'habituated to sexual intercourse' must not be made; State of Jharkhand v. Shailendra Kumar Rai @ Pandav Rai (Supreme Court, 31 October 2022) — reiterated the ban; any person conducting the two-finger test shall be guilty of misconduct; directed removal of the test from medical curricula and workshops for health providers; NMC revised CBME curriculum (2022–24) removed virginity tests and two-finger test; (4) examination of the hymen in sexual assault is limited to documenting fresh injuries (tears, bruises, bleeding) as evidence of recent penetration/force, with the understanding that their absence does not negate penetration; (5) in children, hymenal findings (acute transections, healed complete transections of the posterior rim) can be relevant, assessed by trained examiners with colposcopy/photo-documentation (Adams classification).",
[["Hymen types",["Annular (commonest)","Crescentic/semilunar","Cribriform","Septate","Fimbriated","Imperforate","Elastic/fleshy (may not tear)"]],
["Why hymen status is unreliable",[["Intact despite intercourse","Torn without intercourse"],["Elastic, fleshy, dilatable hymen","Accidents, straddle falls"],["Partial penetration","Masturbation, tampons, foreign bodies"],["Small penis, gentle act","Medical instrumentation"],["Pregnancy reported with intact hymen","Ulceration, congenital absence"]]],
["Fresh vs old hymenal tear",[["Feature","Fresh","Old (healed)"],["Bleeding","Present","Absent"],["Swelling, redness, tenderness","Present","Absent"],["Edges","Raw","Rounded notch"],["Time","< 5–10 days","After healing"],["After childbirth","—","Carunculae myrtiformes"]]],
["Legal position",["Lillu v. State of Haryana (2013): two-finger test violates privacy, dignity","MoHFW 2014: no PV for establishing rape; no 'habituation' comments","State of Jharkhand v. Shailendra Kumar Rai (2022): conducting test = misconduct","S. 49 BSA (53A IEA): character/past sexual experience irrelevant to consent","WHO (2018): eliminate virginity testing"]]],
{c:"Traditional signs of virginity and defloration, chiefly hymenal integrity, are scientifically unreliable because the hymen may survive intercourse or tear without it; the two-finger test is banned by the Supreme Court and MoHFW, past sexual history is legally irrelevant to consent, and examination is limited to documenting fresh injuries whose absence does not exclude assault."});

Z("fmt7",3,"f",["Unnatural sexual offences are sexual acts against the order of nature — sodomy (anal intercourse), bestiality (with animals), buccal coitus, tribadism; after the Supreme Court decriminalised consensual adult same-sex acts (Navtej Singh Johar, 2018) and the BNS omitted IPC S. 377, non-consensual anal and oral penetration of a woman is rape (S. 63 BNS), of a child is POCSO, while bestiality and non-consensual acts on men are under other laws; sexual perversions (paraphilias) are deviations of sexual behaviour where gratification is obtained by abnormal means — sadism, masochism, fetishism, exhibitionism, voyeurism, frotteurism, paedophilia, necrophilia, transvestism.","Unnatural offences are about the act and consent; perversions are about the unusual route to sexual arousal — law punishes harm and lack of consent, not difference.",["Sodomy: anal intercourse; examination of passive agent — recent: anal fissures, abrasions, bruises, lacerations, bleeding, semen in anal canal (swabs); habitual: funnel-shaped anus (infundibulum), loss of rugae, smooth/thickened skin, patulous anus, reflex dilatation, keratinised; active agent — faecal matter/lubricant on penis, smegma absent, abrasions.","Legal status: Navtej Singh Johar v. UoI (2018) — S. 377 read down (consensual adult acts decriminalised); BNS 2023 omits S. 377 — non-consensual anal/oral penetration of woman = rape (S. 63); child — POCSO; animals — Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act; gap in law for non-consensual acts against adult men debated.","Paraphilias (DSM-5/ICD-11): sadism (pleasure by inflicting pain — lust murder), masochism (receiving pain — 'sexual asphyxia' risk), fetishism (inanimate objects — undergarments, shoes), transvestic fetishism (cross-dressing), exhibitionism (exposing genitals — S. 79, 296 BNS), voyeurism (peeping — S. 77 BNS), frotteurism (rubbing in crowds), paedophilia (children — POCSO), necrophilia (with corpses), gerontophilia, zoophilia/bestiality, scatologia (obscene calls), urolagnia, coprophilia.","Medicolegal: sadistic injuries (bites, whip marks, burns), autoerotic asphyxia (accidental death, male, ligature with padding, pornography, transvestism), necrophilia (desecration of graves — S. 301 BNS, trespass on burial places), lust murder; forensic psychiatry evaluation."],"S. 377 decriminalised for consenting adults (2018) and omitted in BNS; non-consensual anal/oral on woman = rape."],
"Unnatural sexual offences ('carnal intercourse against the order of nature'): sexual acts that do not involve penile–vaginal intercourse between man and woman; types — (1) sodomy (buggery, anal intercourse) — penetration of the penis into the anus of a male or female; the person who penetrates is the 'active agent' and the receiver the 'passive agent' (pathic); when performed on a child — paederasty; (2) buccal coitus (oral sex — fellatio, cunnilingus); (3) bestiality (zoophilia) — sexual intercourse by a human with an animal (cow, goat, dog, buffalo, hen); (4) tribadism (lesbianism) — sexual gratification between women by mutual friction of genitals or use of artificial penis (dildo); (5) masturbation; (6) other acts. Legal position: IPC S. 377 (1860) punished 'carnal intercourse against the order of nature with any man, woman or animal' with imprisonment for life or up to 10 years — irrespective of consent; Naz Foundation v. Govt. of NCT of Delhi (Delhi High Court, 2009) decriminalised consensual adult homosexual acts; Suresh Kumar Koushal v. Naz Foundation (Supreme Court, 2013) reversed it; Navtej Singh Johar v. Union of India (Supreme Court Constitution Bench, 6 September 2018) — read down S. 377 to decriminalise consensual sexual acts between adults (of any gender) in private, holding that criminalisation violates Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21 (right to privacy, dignity, equality, autonomy — K.S. Puttaswamy 2017); S. 377 remained applicable to non-consensual acts, acts with minors and bestiality; BNS 2023 does not contain any provision corresponding to S. 377 — hence: (a) non-consensual anal or oral penetration (or insertion of objects) on a woman constitutes rape under S. 63 BNS (since 2013 definition includes vagina, mouth, urethra, anus); (b) any sexual act with a child (male or female) — POCSO Act; (c) bestiality — Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act 1960 (and in some proposals for inclusion); (d) non-consensual sexual acts against adult men and transgender persons — currently a gap in BNS (other provisions — assault S. 74, hurt; Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2019 S. 18 — sexual abuse of transgender persons — 6 months–2 years); this omission has been criticised and challenged. Medical examination in sodomy (with consent; on police requisition): (A) passive agent — (1) signs of recent anal intercourse (especially non-consensual or first time, or in children) — abrasions, bruises, lacerations, fissures (often radiating, at 6 and 12 o'clock positions), swelling, tenderness of the anal margin, bleeding, laceration of the anal sphincter or rectal mucosa (in children — can be severe), lubricant, semen in the anal canal and rectum (anal and rectal swabs for spermatozoa, semen markers and DNA; perianal swabs), faecal stains, foreign hair; pain on walking/defecation; (2) signs of habitual passive anal intercourse (traditionally described; not specific, now regarded as unreliable and their use for persecution condemned — e.g., forced anal examinations of suspected homosexuals are prohibited by human rights bodies): funnel-shaped (infundibuliform) depression of the anus, loss of radiating folds/rugae, smooth, thickened, keratinised perianal skin, patulous (gaping) anus, laxity of anal sphincter with reflex anal dilatation (lateral buttock traction test — dilatation > 2 cm — controversial), anal warts, STIs (gonorrhoea, syphilis — chancre, HIV, HPV, herpes); (B) active agent — (1) faecal matter or lubricant on the penis, especially around the corona and frenulum (penile swab); (2) absence of smegma; (3) abrasions, tears of frenulum; (4) foreign hair; (5) STIs; (6) evidence of struggle (scratches, bites) if non-consensual; (C) collect clothing, swabs, blood (DNA reference), nail scrapings. Bestiality: examination of the accused — animal hair on genitals/clothes, animal blood/secretions, injuries (kicks, scratches), and of the animal (by a veterinarian — injuries to genitals, human semen and DNA); STIs from animals (rare). Sexual perversions (paraphilias; DSM-5 'paraphilic disorders' when they cause distress or harm to others): conditions in which sexual arousal and gratification depend on fantasies or behaviours involving unusual objects, situations or individuals; types — (1) sadism — sexual gratification derived from inflicting physical or psychological pain, suffering or humiliation on the partner (named after Marquis de Sade); may range from biting, pinching, whipping, burning to mutilation and 'lust murder' (killing during or after sexual act with mutilation of genitals and breasts); medicolegal — bite marks, whip/belt marks, burns, ligature marks, genital injuries, serial killings; (2) masochism — sexual pleasure from being subjected to pain, beating, bondage, humiliation (named after Leopold von Sacher-Masoch); sadomasochism; associated with autoerotic (sexual) asphyxia — self-induced cerebral hypoxia (ligature around neck, plastic bag, inhalants) to enhance sexual arousal during masturbation; usually young males; scene — secluded locked room, ligature with padding to avoid marks, escape mechanism, bondage, mirrors, pornography, female clothing (transvestism), previous evidence of similar practice (worn ligature marks on beams); death accidental (failure of escape mechanism) — must be distinguished from suicide and homicide; (3) fetishism — sexual arousal from inanimate objects (women's undergarments, shoes, hair, gloves) or non-genital body parts (partialism — feet); may lead to theft of undergarments; (4) transvestism (transvestic disorder) — sexual arousal from cross-dressing (wearing clothes of the opposite sex); distinct from transgender identity (not a perversion); (5) exhibitionism — exposing one's genitals to unsuspecting strangers (in public places) for sexual excitement; offence — S. 79 BNS (509 IPC — word, gesture or act intended to insult the modesty of a woman), S. 296 BNS (294 IPC — obscene acts in public); (6) voyeurism (scopophilia, 'peeping Tom') — deriving sexual pleasure from secretly watching others undressing, naked or engaged in sexual activity; offence — S. 77 BNS (354C IPC — voyeurism: 1–3 years first conviction; 3–7 years subsequent); includes capturing/disseminating images; (7) frotteurism — rubbing the genitals against or touching a non-consenting person, usually in crowded places (buses, trains); offence — S. 74–75 BNS (354, 354A IPC — assault/sexual harassment); (8) paedophilia — sexual attraction to prepubertal children (generally < 13 years) by a person ≥ 16 years and at least 5 years older; offences under POCSO; (9) necrophilia — sexual intercourse with or sexual gratification from dead bodies; offence — trespassing on burial places/indignity to corpses (S. 301 BNS / 297 IPC); seen in mortuary workers, grave robbers, sadistic murderers; (10) bestiality/zoophilia — with animals; (11) gerontophilia (elderly partners), urolagnia (urine), coprophilia (faeces), telephone scatologia (obscene phone calls — S. 79 BNS, IT Act), klismaphilia (enemas), incest (sexual intercourse between close relatives — offence under POCSO if minor; S. 64(2)(f) aggravated rape by relative; not separately criminalised for consenting adults in India); (12) bondage, tribadism, sapphism (lesbianism — not a perversion; consensual acts not offences); (13) 'eonism' (old term for cross-dressing), uranism (old term for homosexuality — not a perversion). Medicolegal aspects: perversions may lead to crimes (sexual assault, child abuse, voyeurism, theft of fetish objects, lust murders, necrophilia), accidental deaths (autoerotic asphyxia), and may be relevant in assessment of criminal responsibility (generally not a defence of insanity — persons with paraphilias know the nature of their acts); forensic psychiatric evaluation, treatment (psychotherapy, anti-androgens — cyproterone, GnRH analogues; SSRIs) and sex offender management.",
[["Unnatural sexual offences",[["Act","Description","Current law"],["Sodomy","Anal intercourse","Consensual adults — not offence (Navtej 2018); on woman non-consensual — rape (S. 63 BNS); child — POCSO"],["Buccal coitus","Oral sex","Non-consensual on woman — rape; child — POCSO"],["Bestiality","With animals","PCA Act 1960"],["Tribadism","Between women","Consensual — not offence"]]],
["Examination in sodomy",[["Passive agent","Active agent"],["Recent: fissures, abrasions, bruises, lacerations, bleeding","Faecal matter/lubricant on penis"],["Semen in anal canal (swabs)","Absence of smegma"],["STIs","Frenular tears, abrasions"],["'Habitual' signs (funnel anus, lost rugae) — unreliable","Scratches/bites if non-consensual"]]],
["Paraphilias",[["Perversion","Feature","Medicolegal/legal"],["Sadism","Inflicting pain","Bites, whip marks, lust murder"],["Masochism","Receiving pain","Autoerotic asphyxia (accidental death)"],["Fetishism","Inanimate objects","Theft of undergarments"],["Transvestic disorder","Cross-dressing arousal","Autoerotic scenes"],["Exhibitionism","Exposing genitals","S. 79, 296 BNS"],["Voyeurism","Watching secretly","S. 77 BNS"],["Frotteurism","Rubbing in crowds","S. 74–75 BNS"],["Paedophilia","Children","POCSO"],["Necrophilia","Corpses","S. 301 BNS"],["Bestiality","Animals","PCA Act"]]],
["Legal milestones",["IPC S. 377 (1860)","Naz Foundation (Delhi HC 2009)","Koushal (SC 2013) — reversed","Navtej Singh Johar (SC 2018) — consensual adult acts decriminalised","BNS 2023 — no S. 377 equivalent"]]],
{c:"Unnatural sexual offences such as sodomy, buccal coitus and bestiality are now governed by consent and age — consensual adult acts are lawful after Navtej Singh Johar and the BNS omits S. 377, while non-consensual anal or oral penetration of a woman is rape and any act with a child falls under POCSO; paraphilias like sadism, masochism, voyeurism, exhibitionism, paedophilia and necrophilia matter forensically when they cause harm, crime or accidental autoerotic deaths."});

Z("fmt7",4,"f",["Infanticide is the unlawful killing of a newborn child (often by the mother); the medicolegal questions are whether the child was born alive, whether it was viable (≥ 28 weeks/1000 g), the age (maturity), the duration of survival and the cause of death; live birth is proved by signs of respiration — expanded, spongy, crepitant lungs that float (hydrostatic test, positive Fodere's test), increased lung weight (Ploucquet's test), air in stomach and middle ear (Breslau's second life test) — and by changes in umbilical cord, circulation and food in stomach.","The central question is 'Did the baby breathe?' — lungs that breathed fill with air and float; lungs that never breathed are solid and sink.",["Legal: no separate infanticide offence in India — murder (S. 101/103 BNS), concealment of birth by secret disposal (S. 94 BNS / 318 IPC), exposure and abandonment (S. 93 / 317), causing death of quick unborn child (S. 91 / 316); killing a living child of which any part has been brought forth may be culpable homicide (Explanation 3 to S. 100 BNS / 299 IPC).","Stillbirth (died in utero after 28 weeks — signs of maceration, no respiration) vs deadborn (died before birth, may show maceration/mummification) vs live born; viability — 210 days (7 months) gestation, weight ≥ 1–1.5 kg, length ≥ 35 cm, ossification centre in lower femur (36 wk).","Signs of live birth: chest — barrel-shaped, diaphragm at 6th–7th rib (vs 3rd–4th in stillborn); lungs — voluminous, cover heart, margins rounded, pinkish mottled, spongy, crepitant, weight doubles (~ 60 g → 60–70 g; Ploucquet: ratio of lung to body weight 1/70 vs 1/35 after breathing), float in water (hydrostatic test); microscopy — expanded alveoli; air in stomach/intestine (Breslau's test), middle ear; umbilical cord changes (drying 12–24 h, red ring at base by 36–48 h, falls 5–6 days), closure of foramen ovale/ductus (days), meconium passage, food in stomach.","Hydrostatic test: lungs with heart → separately → each lobe → small pieces squeezed; floating pieces after squeezing = air respired (live birth); fallacies — putrefaction (gas — floats even if unrespired; but squeezing expels putrefactive gas), artificial respiration, frozen lungs; sinking despite breathing — atelectasis, pneumonia, hyaline membrane disease, partial respiration.","Causes of death: natural (prematurity, birth asphyxia, congenital anomalies, haemorrhage), accidental (precipitate labour, cord around neck, suffocation by membranes), criminal — acts of commission (smothering — most common, strangulation, drowning, head injury, poisoning, cut throat, burying alive) and omission (failure to tie cord, not providing warmth, food, or clearing airway)."],"Lungs float after squeezing → live birth; fallacies: putrefaction, artificial respiration."],
"Infanticide is the deliberate killing of a newborn infant; in English law (Infanticide Act 1938) it is a specific lesser offence when a woman kills her child under 12 months while the balance of her mind is disturbed by childbirth or lactation; in India there is no separate offence of infanticide — killing a newborn is murder (S. 101/103 BNS; S. 300/302 IPC) or culpable homicide; related offences — S. 91 BNS (316 IPC) causing death of a quick unborn child by an act amounting to culpable homicide; S. 92 (315) act done with intent to prevent a child being born alive or to cause it to die after birth; S. 93 (317) exposure and abandonment of a child under 12 years by parent or person having care; S. 94 (318) concealment of birth by secret burying or otherwise disposing of the dead body of a child, whether it died before, after or during birth; female foeticide and infanticide related to son preference (PCPNDT Act). Legal concept of live birth: for the offence of homicide, the child must be born alive; in Indian law (Explanation 3 to S. 299 IPC / S. 100 BNS) — 'the causing of the death of a child in the mother's womb is not homicide; but it may amount to culpable homicide to cause the death of a living child, if any part of that child has been brought forth, though the child may not have breathed or been completely born'; thus a child partly born and alive is a person; for civil purposes (inheritance), a child born alive with any sign of life (cry, heartbeat, movement). Questions in an alleged infanticide autopsy: (1) Is the body that of a newborn? (2) What was its intrauterine age/maturity — was it viable? (3) Was it born alive or dead (stillborn)? (4) If born alive, how long did it survive? (5) What was the cause of death — natural, accidental or criminal? (6) Identity of mother (examination of the alleged mother for signs of recent delivery; DNA). Newborn (neonate): a baby within the first few hours to days after birth — signs: body covered with vernix caseosa (in folds — disappears in 1–2 days), blood stains, umbilical cord fresh, moist, bluish-white, attached to placenta or cut/torn ends (cut — clean, torn — irregular ends), caput succedaneum (scalp swelling — disappears in 1–2 days), meconium in large intestine (passed within 24–48 h), no milk curd in stomach. Viability: the ability of a fetus to live an independent extrauterine existence; legally in India, a child born after 210 days (7 months, 28 weeks) of gestation is considered viable (MTP and RBD rules define stillbirth as fetal death after 28 weeks; WHO uses 22 weeks/500 g for statistics); criteria — weight ≥ 1–1.5 kg (≥ 1000 g), crown–heel length ≥ 35 cm (Haase's rule — length in first 5 months = month²; after 5 months = month × 5; e.g., 7 months = 35 cm, 9 months = 45 cm, full-term 50 cm), crown–rump length; nails reaching fingertips, scalp hair, testes descended (7th–9th month), ossification centres — calcaneum (5th–6th month), talus (7th month), lower end of femur (9th month — 36 weeks — sign of maturity), upper end of tibia (at birth), cuboid (at birth); a non-viable fetus cannot be the subject of infanticide in the usual sense (but abortion laws apply). Stillbirth and deadborn: stillborn — a child that has issued forth from its mother after the 28th week of pregnancy and which did not at any time after being completely expelled breathe or show any other signs of life; deadborn — a child that died in utero (before birth) and shows signs of death in utero — rigor mortis, maceration (aseptic autolysis — begins within 12 h of intrauterine death: skin slippage and reddish discolouration (24 h), bullae with serous/bloody fluid, sweetish rancid smell, flaccid body with overriding skull bones — Spalding's sign on X-ray (7 days), Robert's sign (gas in great vessels), hyperflexion of spine), or mummification (fetus papyraceus); a macerated fetus could not have been born alive. Signs of live birth (proof of respiration and extrauterine life): (A) external — chest is arched/barrel-shaped (vs flat) with increased circumference; the umbilical cord changes; caput disappearing; (B) internal — respiratory system: (1) position of diaphragm — after respiration the dome of the diaphragm descends to the level of 6th–7th rib (before respiration — at 3rd–4th rib); (2) lungs — volume: after respiration lungs expand and fill the thoracic cavity, covering the heart and the thymus partially; before respiration lungs are small, collapsed, retracted towards the vertebral column, posterior; margins: rounded/blunt after respiration vs sharp edges before; colour: mottled, marbled pink/bright red after vs uniform reddish-brown/purple (liver-like) before; consistency: spongy, elastic, crepitant on pressure vs firm, dense, non-crepitant, liver-like; on section: frothy blood-stained fluid exudes vs only blood oozes, no froth; (3) weight of lungs — increases after respiration due to increased blood flow through pulmonary circulation: before respiration combined weight ~ 30–40 g, after respiration ~ 60–70 g; Ploucquet's test (fetal lung–body weight ratio): before respiration 1 : 70; after respiration 1 : 35 — unreliable; (4) specific gravity — before respiration 1.04–1.05 (sinks), after respiration 0.94 (floats) — basis of hydrostatic test; (5) microscopic examination — alveoli expanded, lined by flattened epithelium, bronchioles dilated vs collapsed alveoli lined by cuboidal epithelium, folded, with amniotic squames/meconium (aspirated in utero) in unrespired lungs; hyaline membranes indicate survival for several hours; (C) hydrostatic test (Fodere's test, flotation test, docimasia pulmonum): procedure — the trachea is ligated (to prevent escape/entry of air), the thoracic organs (lungs with heart and thymus) removed en bloc and placed in a large container of clean cold water: (i) if they float — lungs contain air; (ii) the lungs are separated from heart and thymus and floated individually; (iii) each lobe tested separately; (iv) each lobe cut into 12–20 small pieces and floated; (v) the pieces are squeezed hard between the fingers (or under a weight) wrapped in cloth and re-floated: if they still float, air was introduced by respiration (residual air cannot be expelled by squeezing); if they sink after squeezing, the air was due to putrefaction (gas in interstitial tissues is expelled) or artificial insufflation; interpretation — floating of most pieces after squeezing indicates live birth; sinking of all pieces indicates no respiration (stillbirth/no breathing); fallacies — (a) unrespired lungs may float due to — putrefaction (gas formation; sub-pleural bullae; differentiated by squeezing; also check other organs for putrefaction), artificial respiration (mouth-to-mouth/Schultze's swinging — usually inflates stomach more than lungs; partial inflation), emphysema; (b) respired lungs may sink due to — atelectasis (feeble respiration in premature/weak infants — air absorbed after death if breathing stopped and the baby survived a while), pneumonia, hyaline membrane disease, congenital syphilis (white pneumonia), lung consolidation, oedema; (c) child may breathe in utero (vagitus uterinus — after rupture of membranes — air entering uterus) or during birth (vagitus vaginalis — head in vagina — child breathed but died before complete birth — not legally live birth if dies before wholly born, but Explanation 3 applies); hydrostatic test is unnecessary when the body shows signs of maceration or when food in stomach proves live birth; (D) gastrointestinal — Breslau's second life test (stomach–bowel test): air is swallowed with breathing and appears in the stomach (within minutes), small intestine (1–2 h), large intestine (5–6 h), and rectum (12 h); stomach and intestines are ligated at both ends, removed and placed in water — floating indicates air swallowed (live birth and duration of survival); fallacies — putrefaction, artificial respiration; presence of milk, food or colostrum in the stomach is conclusive proof of live birth; meconium passed (complete evacuation 24–48 h) — not conclusive (may be passed in utero in distress); (E) middle ear (Wreden's test) — before birth middle ear contains gelatinous embryonic connective tissue (myxoid); after respiration and crying, air enters the middle ear through the Eustachian tube and the gelatinous material gradually disappears; not reliable; (F) circulatory changes (indicate survival period) — closure of umbilical arteries (begins within minutes, complete by 3rd day — obliterated), umbilical vein (by 4th–5th day — becomes ligamentum teres), ductus venosus (by 5th day–2 weeks), ductus arteriosus (functional closure 10–15 h; anatomical closure 2–3 weeks), foramen ovale (functional closure at birth; anatomical closure months — patency may persist); (G) changes in the umbilical cord (survival time) — the stump dries and shrivels from the cut end (mummification) within 12–24 hours; a red inflammatory ring (line of demarcation) appears at the base (at junction with skin) within 36–48 hours; the cord falls off on the 5th–6th day leaving a raw area that heals by the 10th–12th day forming a scar; (H) skin changes — vernix disappears, skin colour changes from bright red (birth) to brick red (2–3 days) to yellowish (physiological jaundice — 3–5 days); desquamation of skin (exfoliation) — days 2–5; (I) caput succedaneum disappears in 24–36 h; cephalhaematoma persists weeks; (J) blood changes — fetal haemoglobin, nucleated RBCs disappear within days; (K) evidence of feeding, clothing, washing, wrapping. Causes of death in newborns: (1) natural — prematurity, immaturity, birth asphyxia (placental insufficiency, cord prolapse), haemorrhage (intracranial from birth trauma, haemorrhagic disease of newborn — vitamin K deficiency), congenital anomalies (anencephaly, heart defects), infections (pneumonia, septicaemia, syphilis), erythroblastosis fetalis, respiratory distress syndrome; (2) accidental — during labour: prolonged labour, cord compression or prolapse, cord around the neck (may leave a mark simulating strangulation — no bruising), birth injuries (skull fractures from forceps, cephalhaematoma, tentorial tear), precipitate labour (sudden delivery while standing or squatting on toilet — head injury, drowning in toilet/latrine), suffocation by membranes ('caul' over face), by blood/meconium aspiration; after birth: overlaying, suffocation in bedding, haemorrhage from untied cord; (3) criminal (infanticide) — (a) acts of commission: smothering (most common — hand over nose and mouth, pillow; minimal signs), strangulation (ligature — cord around neck deliberately, or fingers — throttling), drowning (water bucket, well, latrine — diatoms), blows on the head (skull fractures, crushing), fractures of cervical spine (twisting neck), cut throat, stab wounds, burns and scalds, poisoning (opium — 'giving afeem', tobacco, arsenic, salt, oleander, calotropis juice, datura), choking with cloth/mud/grain/tobacco in mouth, exposure to cold, burying alive, dropping in rivers, pins into fontanelles/heart/brain, breaking neck; female infanticide in some communities (drowning in milk — 'doodh peeti', feeding paddy husk, poisoning with plant extracts); (b) acts of omission (negligence): failure to tie the cord (haemorrhage), failure to clear the airways (suffocation in membranes/fluid), failure to protect from cold or heat, failure to feed, failure to remove the child from the mother's discharges, abandonment; (4) examination of the mother — signs of recent delivery (breasts, abdomen, genitals, lochia — fmt10), age, mental state (postpartum psychosis), DNA comparison with infant.",
[["Medicolegal questions",["Is it a newborn?","Viable? (≥ 28 wk, ≥ 1 kg, ≥ 35 cm, lower femur ossification)","Born alive or dead?","Duration of survival","Cause of death — natural, accidental, criminal","Identity of mother"]],
["Unrespired vs respired lungs",[["Feature","Before respiration","After respiration"],["Diaphragm level","3rd–4th rib","6th–7th rib"],["Volume","Small, posterior, don't cover heart","Voluminous, cover heart"],["Margins","Sharp","Rounded"],["Colour","Uniform reddish-brown (liver-like)","Mottled pink"],["Consistency","Firm, non-crepitant","Spongy, crepitant"],["Cut surface","Blood, no froth","Frothy blood"],["Weight","~30–40 g","~60–70 g"],["Ploucquet ratio","1 : 70","1 : 35"],["Specific gravity","1.04–1.05 (sinks)","0.94 (floats)"],["Microscopy","Collapsed alveoli, cuboidal lining","Expanded alveoli, flat lining"]]],
["Hydrostatic test",[["Aspect","Details"],["Steps","Tie trachea → organs en bloc → lungs separately → lobes → pieces → squeeze and re-float"],["Float after squeezing","Respired (live birth)"],["Sink after squeezing","Unrespired (or putrefactive gas expelled)"],["False float","Putrefaction, artificial respiration"],["False sink","Atelectasis, pneumonia, HMD, partial breathing"],["Not needed","Maceration (deadborn); milk in stomach (live born)"]]],
["Survival period indicators",[["Change","Time"],["Air in stomach/small intestine/large intestine","Minutes / 1–2 h / 5–6 h (Breslau)"],["Cord dries","12–24 h"],["Red ring at cord base","36–48 h"],["Cord falls","5–6 days"],["Ductus arteriosus anatomical closure","2–3 weeks"],["Skin desquamation","2–5 days"]]],
["Causes of death",[["Natural","Accidental","Criminal"],["Prematurity, anomalies","Cord around neck/prolapse","Smothering (commonest)"],["Birth asphyxia","Precipitate labour (head injury, drowning)","Strangulation, drowning"],["Infections, HDN","Suffocation by membranes","Head injury, poisoning"],["RDS","Overlaying","Omission: untied cord, no warmth/food"]]]],
{i:"Proof of live birth rests chiefly on signs of respiration, interpreted with care for fallacies.",x:[["Signs of maceration (deadborn)",["Skin slippage, reddish discolouration (24 h)","Bullae, sweetish smell","Flaccid body, overriding skull bones","Spalding's sign (X-ray ~7 days)","Robert's sign (gas in great vessels)"]]],f:"Newborn body → viable? → maceration present → deadborn (no infanticide)\n No maceration → lungs: expanded, crepitant, float after squeezing → live birth → survival (Breslau, cord, circulation) → cause of death (natural / accidental / commission / omission)",c:"In suspected infanticide the autopsy establishes that the body is a viable newborn, proves live birth chiefly by signs of respiration — expanded crepitant lungs, low specific gravity and a positive hydrostatic test after squeezing, supported by air in the gut and food in the stomach — estimates survival from cord and circulatory changes, and identifies natural, accidental or criminal causes of death, bearing in mind fallacies such as putrefaction and atelectasis."});

Z("fmt7",5,"f",["Battered baby syndrome (non-accidental injury, child physical abuse; Caffey 1946, Kempe 1962) is repeated physical injury to a child (usually < 3 years) by parents or caregivers, recognised by multiple injuries of different ages inconsistent with the history — bruises (grip, slap, bite, belt marks), burns (cigarette, immersion), multiple fractures in different stages of healing (metaphyseal corner/bucket-handle fractures, posterior rib fractures), and shaken baby syndrome (subdural haematoma, retinal haemorrhages, encephalopathy); doctors must suspect, protect, document and report.","A battered child's body is a calendar of abuse — old and new injuries side by side that no fall or accident can explain.",["Features of history: delay in seeking care, vague/inconsistent/changing history, injury inconsistent with developmental age ('fell from bed'), blaming sibling, repeated visits to different hospitals, parental indifference/hostility.","Skin: bruises of different ages (colours), on unusual sites (ears, cheeks, neck, buttocks, genitals, back), fingertip/grip marks on arms/chest, slap marks (parallel lines), pattern marks (belt, cord loop, hand), bite marks (adult — intercanine distance > 3 cm), torn frenulum (forced feeding), cigarette burns (round 5–10 mm), immersion scalds (glove/stocking, sparing flexures — 'doughnut' sparing of buttocks), alopecia (hair pulling).","Skeletal: multiple fractures in various stages of healing; classic metaphyseal lesions (corner and bucket-handle fractures — from shaking/pulling limbs — highly specific), posterior rib fractures (squeezing), spiral fractures of long bones in non-ambulant infants, skull fractures (complex, bilateral), vertebral, scapular, sternal fractures; skeletal survey (X-ray) ± bone scan; exclude osteogenesis imperfecta, rickets, scurvy, Menkes.","Shaken baby syndrome/abusive head trauma: violent shaking → acceleration–deceleration → bridging vein tear → SDH, diffuse retinal haemorrhages (multilayered), encephalopathy/cerebral oedema; ± rib/metaphyseal fractures; no external injury often; visceral injuries (liver, duodenal haematoma, pancreas); poisoning, starvation (failure to thrive); Munchausen syndrome by proxy (fabricated illness).","Management/legal: admit and protect, treat, document with photographs and body chart, skeletal survey, ophthalmology, CT/MRI, coagulation profile, inform police/Child Welfare Committee (JJ Act 2015 — S. 75 cruelty to child: up to 3 years), Childline 1098; POCSO if sexual; differentiate from accidental injury, birth trauma, bleeding disorders, cultural practices (cupping, coining), Mongolian spots."],"Multiple injuries of different ages + inconsistent history = suspect abuse; SDH + retinal haemorrhages + encephalopathy = shaken baby."],
"Battered baby syndrome (battered child syndrome; non-accidental injury (NAI) of childhood; child physical abuse; 'Caffey–Kempe syndrome'; Maltreatment syndrome) refers to a clinical condition in young children who have received serious physical abuse, generally from a parent, step-parent, foster-parent or caregiver, repeatedly; history — John Caffey (1946, paediatric radiologist) described the association of subdural haematoma with multiple long-bone fractures in infants; C. Henry Kempe et al. (1962) coined the term 'battered child syndrome'; Caffey (1972) described the 'whiplash shaken infant syndrome'; Ambroise Tardieu (1860) earlier documented child abuse deaths in France. Child abuse (WHO) encompasses all forms of physical and/or emotional ill-treatment, sexual abuse, neglect or negligent treatment, or commercial or other exploitation resulting in actual or potential harm to the child's health, survival, development or dignity; types — physical abuse, sexual abuse, emotional (psychological) abuse, neglect (failure to provide food, shelter, medical care, education, supervision), fabricated or induced illness (Munchausen syndrome by proxy — caregiver, usually mother, fabricates or induces illness in the child to obtain medical attention — poisoning with salt, laxatives, insulin; suffocation; contamination of urine samples), child labour, trafficking, child marriage. Risk factors: (a) child — age < 3 years (most victims; infants < 1 year at highest risk of fatal abuse), prematurity, low birth weight, disability, chronic illness, unwanted child, excessive crying (colic), stepchild, female child in some cultures; (b) parent/caregiver — young or single parents, history of being abused as a child (cycle of violence), substance abuse, mental illness (depression, personality disorders), low intelligence, poverty, unemployment, social isolation, domestic violence, unrealistic expectations of the child, step-parents/boyfriends (paramours); (c) environment — overcrowding, stress, lack of support. Clinical features — (I) history (red flags): delay in seeking medical care; history absent, vague, inconsistent, or changing with each telling and among caregivers; mechanism described is inconsistent with the type, severity, pattern or age of injuries ('fell from the sofa' with multiple fractures); injury inconsistent with the child's developmental stage (e.g., an infant who cannot roll 'fell off the bed'); history of repeated injuries, multiple hospital visits (hospital shopping); blaming the child or sibling; abnormal parent–child interaction — parents indifferent, hostile, unconcerned, leave hospital; child withdrawn, fearful, 'frozen watchfulness', no tears, wary of adults; (II) physical findings — (1) skin and soft tissue (most common — 90%): bruises in various stages of healing (different colours), clustered, on unusual sites — face (cheeks, ears — 'pinch marks'), neck, upper arms, chest, back, buttocks, abdomen, genitals, inner thighs, soles (accidental bruises in active toddlers occur over bony prominences — shins, forehead, knees, elbows); 'TEN-4' rule — bruising to the Torso, Ears, Neck in child < 4 years or any bruise in an infant < 4 months is suspicious; grip marks (fingertip bruises — 1–2 cm oval — on arms, chest from squeezing/shaking), slap marks (parallel linear bruises with intervening pale lines — outline of fingers), pattern bruises (belt, belt buckle, cord loops — U-shaped, hangers, sticks, spoon, shoe), bite marks (human adult bites — oval/elliptical with intercanine distance > 3 cm; child's < 3 cm), ligature marks on wrists/ankles/neck (tying), torn frenulum of upper lip (forced feeding, blow to mouth — in non-ambulant infant highly suspicious), black eyes (bilateral), traumatic alopecia (hair pulling with subgaleal haematoma), lacerations; (2) burns and scalds (~ 10%): cigarette burns (circular, 5–10 mm, punched-out, uniform depth, multiple, on palms, soles, back, buttocks), contact burns from hot objects (iron, grill, heater — patterned, sharply demarcated), immersion (dipping) scalds — forced immersion in hot water: 'glove and stocking' distribution with sharp tide-lines, symmetric, uniform depth, sparing of flexural creases (child flexes in self-protection) and of the buttocks pressed against the cool tub bottom ('doughnut sign'), no splash marks (vs accidental scalds — irregular, splash marks, asymmetrical, on front), branding, chemical burns, electrical burns; (3) skeletal injuries (~ 25–50%) — fractures in a child less than 18 months are suspicious, especially if non-ambulant; multiple fractures in various stages of healing (fresh, callus formation, remodelled) are the hallmark; highly specific fractures: (a) classic metaphyseal lesions (CML) — corner fractures and bucket-handle fractures of the metaphyses of long bones (distal femur, proximal and distal tibia, proximal humerus) caused by violent shaking (flailing limbs) or pulling/twisting of limbs — planar fractures through the primary spongiosa; (b) posterior rib fractures (near the costovertebral junction) — caused by squeezing the chest with both hands (antero-posterior compression) while shaking; multiple, bilateral, symmetrical, often visible only when healing (callus 'string of beads'); (c) fractures of scapula, sternum, spinous processes, vertebral bodies (compression); (d) complex/bilateral/depressed skull fractures and fractures crossing sutures; moderately specific — multiple fractures, bilateral fractures, digital fractures, fractures of different ages, epiphyseal separation; common but less specific — clavicle, long-bone shaft fractures (spiral fractures in non-ambulatory children — twisting), linear skull fracture; subperiosteal new bone formation (periosteal reaction) along shafts (from repeated traction); (4) head injury (abusive head trauma — most common cause of death and morbidity in child abuse): (a) shaken baby syndrome (SBS)/whiplash shaken infant syndrome — violent to-and-fro shaking of an infant (usually < 1 year — weak neck muscles, large heavy head, large subarachnoid spaces, unmyelinated brain) held by the chest or arms, typically by a frustrated caregiver responding to persistent crying; repeated acceleration–deceleration and rotational forces tear bridging veins and cause axonal injury; classic triad — (i) subdural haematoma (often thin, bilateral, interhemispheric, of different ages), (ii) retinal haemorrhages (extensive, multilayered — pre-retinal, intra-retinal, sub-retinal — extending to the periphery (ora serrata), retinoschisis, perimacular folds — highly suggestive), and (iii) encephalopathy (cerebral oedema, hypoxic-ischaemic injury — irritability, lethargy, seizures, vomiting, apnoea, coma); often with no external signs of injury; associated posterior rib fractures and metaphyseal fractures, grip bruises on the chest or arms, optic nerve sheath haemorrhage, spinal cord injury (cervical); (b) shaken impact syndrome (shaking + impact against a surface — skull fracture, scalp bruising); (c) direct blows — skull fractures, EDH, contusions; (5) abdominal/visceral injuries (second leading cause of death) — blows/kicks to the abdomen: liver laceration, splenic rupture, duodenal haematoma (intramural — vomiting), pancreatic injury (pseudocyst), mesenteric tears, bowel perforation, renal injury; may be minimal external bruising; (6) other — oral injuries (dental fractures, palatal injuries), eye injuries, ear (bruising of pinna — 'boxing ears'), poisoning (intentional), drowning, suffocation; failure to thrive/malnutrition (neglect — weight below 3rd centile; gains weight in hospital), poor hygiene, untreated medical conditions, dehydration; signs of sexual abuse (POCSO); (III) investigations — complete physical examination with body chart and photographs (with scale, colour), skeletal survey (standardised radiographs of all bones — mandatory in children < 2 years with suspected abuse; repeated after 11–14 days to detect healing fractures not initially visible) and/or radionuclide bone scan, CT and MRI of head (SDH of different ages), ophthalmological examination (dilated fundoscopy by an ophthalmologist — retinal haemorrhages), abdominal imaging, liver and pancreatic enzymes, urinalysis, coagulation profile (platelet count, PT, aPTT, fibrinogen, von Willebrand — to exclude bleeding disorders), bone profile (calcium, phosphate, ALP, vitamin D, PTH — rickets), copper (Menkes), genetic tests for osteogenesis imperfecta (blue sclerae, Wormian bones, family history), toxicology screen, examination of siblings; (IV) differential diagnosis (conditions mimicking abuse) — accidental injuries, birth trauma (clavicle fracture, cephalhaematoma, retinal haemorrhages in newborns — resolve within weeks), bleeding disorders (haemophilia, ITP, vitamin K deficiency, leukaemia — easy bruising), osteogenesis imperfecta, rickets, scurvy, congenital syphilis, Menkes disease, copper deficiency, Caffey disease (infantile cortical hyperostosis), Mongolian spots (bluish-grey sacral patches — mistaken for bruises), cultural practices (coining, cupping, moxibustion, branding as folk medicine), dermatological conditions (phytophotodermatitis, impetigo mimicking cigarette burns, erythema multiforme), glutaric aciduria type I (SDH), benign enlargement of subarachnoid spaces, CPR artefacts (anterior rib fractures rare). Autopsy findings in fatal cases: multiple external injuries of different ages (histological dating), SDH and retinal haemorrhages (eyes removed for examination), fractures (radiography before autopsy; histology of fracture callus for dating), visceral injuries, malnutrition (absence of subcutaneous fat, small thymus — stress involution), dehydration, poor hygiene (nappy rash), signs of sexual abuse. Medicolegal aspects and management: (1) suspicion is the key — any doctor seeing an injured child must consider abuse; (2) protection — admit the child to hospital (place of safety) even for minor injuries; treat injuries; protect siblings; (3) documentation — meticulous recording of history (verbatim from each caregiver and the child), examination findings, photographs, investigations; (4) reporting — inform police and the Child Welfare Committee (CWC) under the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act 2015 (child in need of care and protection); Childline (1098); for sexual abuse — mandatory reporting under POCSO S. 19; (5) legal provisions — JJ Act 2015 S. 75 — punishment for cruelty to child by a person having actual charge or control (assaults, abandons, abuses, exposes or wilfully neglects the child causing unnecessary mental or physical suffering) — imprisonment up to 3 years and/or fine ₹1 lakh; up to 5 years if by an organisation; if the child is physically incapacitated or mentally ill — up to 10 years; S. 82 — corporal punishment in child care institutions; RTE Act 2009 S. 17 — prohibition of physical punishment and mental harassment in schools; BNS — hurt, grievous hurt, murder, S. 93 (exposure and abandonment); (6) multidisciplinary team — paediatrician, forensic expert, radiologist, ophthalmologist, social worker, psychologist, police, CWC; (7) prognosis — high risk of repeated abuse (recurrence ~ 30–50%) and death if the child is returned to the abusive environment without intervention; long-term physical, cognitive and psychological sequelae.",
[["Red flags in history",["Delay in seeking care","Vague, inconsistent, changing history","Injury inconsistent with mechanism or development","Repeated injuries, hospital shopping","Blaming child/sibling","Indifferent/hostile parents; 'frozen watchfulness' in child"]],
["Physical findings",[["System","Suspicious findings"],["Skin","Bruises of different ages on ears, neck, torso, buttocks; grip, slap, belt, bite marks; torn frenulum"],["Burns","Cigarette burns; immersion scalds (glove-stocking, sparing flexures, doughnut sign)"],["Skeleton","Multiple fractures of different ages; metaphyseal corner/bucket-handle; posterior rib fractures; spiral fractures in non-ambulant"],["Head","SDH (various ages), skull fractures"],["Eyes","Multilayered retinal haemorrhages"],["Abdomen","Liver/spleen rupture, duodenal haematoma, pancreatic injury"],["General","Failure to thrive, neglect"]]],
["Shaken baby syndrome",[["Aspect","Details"],["Age","Usually < 1 year"],["Mechanism","Violent shaking → acceleration–deceleration, rotation → bridging vein tears, axonal injury"],["Triad","SDH + retinal haemorrhages + encephalopathy"],["Associated","Posterior rib fractures, metaphyseal fractures, grip bruises"],["External injury","Often absent"]]],
["Investigations",["Body chart + photographs","Skeletal survey (repeat at 11–14 days) ± bone scan","CT/MRI brain","Dilated fundoscopy","Coagulation profile, bone profile","Toxicology; examine siblings"]],
["Differential diagnosis",["Accidental injury, birth trauma","Bleeding disorders, leukaemia","Osteogenesis imperfecta, rickets, scurvy, Menkes","Mongolian spots","Cultural practices (cupping, coining)","Glutaric aciduria type I"]],
["Management and law",["Admit and protect; treat","Document meticulously","Report to police, Child Welfare Committee; Childline 1098","JJ Act 2015 S. 75 — cruelty to child","POCSO for sexual abuse","Multidisciplinary team"]]],
{c:"Battered baby syndrome is repeated non-accidental injury of young children by caregivers, suspected when the history is delayed or inconsistent and injuries of different ages appear — patterned bruises, cigarette or immersion burns, metaphyseal and posterior rib fractures, and the shaken baby triad of subdural haematoma, retinal haemorrhages and encephalopathy; doctors must protect, investigate with a skeletal survey and fundoscopy, exclude mimics, document and report under the JJ Act and POCSO."});

Z("fmt8",0,"f",["A poison is any substance which, when introduced into or applied to the body by any route, is capable of causing ill health, injury or death by its chemical or physical action; poisons are classified by their mode of action — corrosives (strong acids, alkalis), irritants (inorganic — metallic like arsenic, lead; non-metallic — phosphorus; organic — plant like castor, croton; animal — snake, insects; mechanical — glass), systemic poisons acting on CNS (somniferous — opium; inebriants — alcohol; deliriants — datura; convulsants — strychnine), CVS (oleander, aconite), lungs (asphyxiants — CO, cyanide), and miscellaneous; diagnosis of poisoning in the living rests on history, symptoms, signs, chemical analysis of vomit/blood/urine, and in the dead on autopsy findings and chemical analysis of preserved viscera.","Poisons are grouped by what they do — burn (corrosive), inflame (irritant), or silently disturb a system (brain, heart, lungs); diagnosis links the story, the signs and the lab.",["Classification (Modi/Reddy): corrosives — strong acids (H₂SO₄, HNO₃, HCl), organic acids (carbolic, oxalic), alkalis (NaOH, KOH); irritants — inorganic (non-metallic: phosphorus, iodine; metallic: arsenic, mercury, lead, copper, antimony, zinc), organic (vegetable: abrus, castor, croton, calotropis, semecarpus; animal: snake, scorpion, insects), mechanical (powdered glass, diamond dust, hair); systemic — neurotic (cerebral: somniferous — opium, inebriants — alcohol, ether, chloroform, barbiturates, deliriants — datura, cannabis, cocaine; spinal: strychnine, gelsemium; peripheral: curare, conium), cardiac (digitalis, oleander, aconite, nicotine, tobacco), asphyxiants (CO, CO₂, H₂S, war gases), miscellaneous (analgesics, antipyretics, antihistaminics, tranquillisers, food poisoning).","Factors modifying action: dose, form (gas > liquid > solid; solubility), route (IV > inhalation > IM > SC > oral > rectal > skin), age (children, elderly sensitive), idiosyncrasy, tolerance (opium, alcohol, arsenic), cumulative action (lead, digitalis), state of health, sleep, synergism/antagonism, condition of stomach.","Diagnosis in living: history (sudden onset in healthy person, symptoms after food/drink, several persons affected), toxidromes (cholinergic, anticholinergic, sympathomimetic, opioid, sedative), smell of breath (garlic — arsenic, phosphorus, OPC; bitter almonds — cyanide; kerosene; fruity/acetone — DKA, isopropanol; carbolic), colour of urine, investigations — chemical analysis of vomit, gastric lavage, blood, urine; serial observations; secret watching.","Diagnosis in dead: external — PM staining colour (cherry-red CO, brown nitrites), smell, corrosion of lips/mouth, needle marks; internal — mucosal changes of stomach (corrosion, inflammation, haemorrhage, perforation), smell of contents, colour, crystals/pills, congestion of organs, liver/kidney necrosis; preserve viscera; histopathology; chemical analysis report (FSL); circumstantial evidence (container, suicide note)."],"Corrosives burn; irritants inflame; systemic poisons act on brain, spinal cord, heart, lungs."],
"Toxicology (Greek — toxicon, arrow poison) is the science dealing with properties, actions, toxicity, fatal dose, detection, estimation, interpretation of results of toxicological analysis, and management of poisons; forensic toxicology deals with medicolegal aspects of poisoning (suicidal, homicidal, accidental), clinical toxicology with diagnosis and treatment. Poison: legally, any substance which, if introduced into the living body or brought into contact with any part thereof, will produce ill health or death by its constitutional or local effects or both; the difference between a medicine and a poison is mainly the dose and intent ('All things are poison and nothing is without poison; only the dose makes a thing not a poison' — Paracelsus); legal provisions — S. 123 BNS (328 IPC) causing hurt by means of poison with intent to commit an offence — up to 10 years; poisoning with intent to murder (S. 103), attempt (S. 109); S. 274–278 BNS (272–276 IPC) adulteration of food, drink and drugs; Drugs and Cosmetics Act 1940; Poisons Act 1919; Insecticides Act 1968; NDPS Act 1985; FSS Act 2006. Classification of poisons (by mode of action and clinical features): (I) corrosives — produce direct local destruction (corrosion, chemical burns) of tissues at the site of contact (mouth, oesophagus, stomach); (a) strong mineral acids — sulphuric acid (oil of vitriol), nitric acid (aqua fortis), hydrochloric acid (muriatic acid); (b) strong organic acids — carbolic acid (phenol), oxalic acid, acetic acid (glacial), formic acid; (c) strong alkalis — sodium hydroxide (caustic soda), potassium hydroxide (caustic potash), ammonium hydroxide, sodium/potassium carbonates; (d) metallic salts — zinc chloride, mercuric chloride, copper sulphate (also irritant), silver nitrate; (II) irritants — produce inflammation of the gastrointestinal mucosa (vomiting, abdominal pain, purging — gastroenteritis); (a) inorganic — (i) non-metallic: phosphorus, chlorine, bromine, iodine, borax; (ii) metallic: arsenic, antimony, mercury, lead, copper, zinc, thallium, chromium, cadmium, barium; (b) organic — (i) vegetable (plant): abrus precatorius (rati), castor (ricinus), croton, calotropis (madar), semecarpus (marking nut), capsicum (chillies), colocynth, ergot; (ii) animal: snake venoms, scorpion, spider, bees, wasps, cantharides (Spanish fly); (c) mechanical: powdered glass, diamond dust, chopped hair, needles, metallic fragments; (III) systemic poisons — absorbed and act on specific organ systems: (a) neurotic — (i) cerebral: somniferous (opium and alkaloids — morphine, codeine, heroin), inebriant (alcohols — ethanol, methanol; general anaesthetics — ether, chloroform; sedatives and hypnotics — barbiturates, benzodiazepines), deliriant (datura, cannabis/hemp, cocaine; atropa belladonna, hyoscyamus); (ii) spinal: convulsants — strychnine (nux vomica), gelsemium; (iii) peripheral nerves: curare, conium (hemlock); (b) cardiac — digitalis, oleanders (yellow and white), aconite, nicotine/tobacco, quinine; (c) asphyxiants (gases) — carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide, hydrogen sulphide, cyanide (HCN), war gases (chlorine, phosgene, mustard), coal gas, sewer gas; (IV) miscellaneous — agrochemicals (insecticides — organophosphates, carbamates, organochlorines, pyrethroids; rodenticides — aluminium/zinc phosphide, warfarin; herbicides — paraquat), household products (kerosene, hydrocarbons), therapeutic drugs (paracetamol, salicylates, antidepressants, iron), food poisoning, poisonous mushrooms, venoms. Other classification — by origin (plant, animal, mineral, synthetic), by target organ (hepatotoxic, nephrotoxic, cardiotoxic), by chemical nature, by use (agrochemical, industrial, household, drug). Factors modifying the action of poisons: (1) dose — larger dose, more rapid and severe; very large doses of irritants may cause vomiting and expulsion; (2) form — gases and vapours act fastest (inhalation), liquids faster than solids; finely powdered faster than lumps; chemical combination (insoluble salts — barium sulphate harmless vs barium carbonate toxic; arsenious oxide vs arsenic sulphide), mechanical mixture; (3) route of administration — in order of rapidity: intravenous > inhalation > intramuscular > subcutaneous > oral (sublingual) > rectal > vaginal > skin (intact); some substances are toxic only by certain routes (snake venom harmless orally (if no ulcers); curare inactive orally); (4) condition of the body — age (children and the elderly more susceptible, except to opium — infants very sensitive — and alcohol), health (renal/hepatic disease impair elimination; poisons act more rapidly), sleep (slows action), intoxication, empty vs full stomach (food delays absorption), idiosyncrasy (abnormal response to normal doses — genetic, e.g., G6PD deficiency with primaquine, succinylcholine apnoea), allergy/hypersensitivity (penicillin), tolerance (habituation reduces effect — opium, alcohol, tobacco, arsenic eaters — 'Styrian arsenic eaters'; not to cocaine), cumulative action (repeated small doses accumulate — lead, mercury, arsenic, digitalis, strychnine); (5) interactions — synergism (alcohol + barbiturates), antagonism; (6) environment (temperature). Diagnosis of poisoning in the living: (A) history — sudden onset of symptoms in a previously healthy individual; symptoms soon after taking food, drink or medicine; several persons partaking the same food affected similarly; symptoms in a person with motive for suicide or a known enemy; presence of containers, empty strips, suicide note; the patient's or witnesses' account (often unreliable); (B) symptoms and signs — characteristic patterns ('toxidromes'): cholinergic (OP — miosis, salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, GI cramps, emesis (SLUDGE), bronchorrhoea, bradycardia, fasciculations), anticholinergic (datura, atropine, antihistamines — 'hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter' — mydriasis, dry skin, flushing, hyperthermia, tachycardia, delirium, urinary retention), sympathomimetic (cocaine, amphetamines — mydriasis, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, sweating, agitation), opioid (pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression, coma), sedative-hypnotic (coma, hypotonia, normal pupils), serotonin syndrome; specific signs — odour of breath (garlic — arsenic, phosphorus, organophosphates (kerosene-like solvent), thallium, selenium; bitter almonds — cyanide; rotten eggs — H₂S; pear-like — chloral hydrate; acetone/fruity — isopropanol, DKA; alcohol; kerosene; phenol — carbolic acid; violets in urine — turpentine; mothballs — naphthalene; shoe polish — nitrobenzene), skin colour (cherry-red — CO; cyanosis; jaundice — paracetamol, phosphorus; chocolate-brown blood — methaemoglobinaemia), pupils (miosis — opiates, OP, clonidine; mydriasis — datura, cocaine, amphetamines, methanol), corrosion of lips/mouth (corrosives), urine colour (dark/green — phenol (carboluria), red — rifampicin, haemoglobinuria, myoglobinuria), vital signs; (C) laboratory investigations — collection of vomit, first gastric lavage aspirate (stomach washings), blood (grey-top fluoride vial for alcohol, EDTA/plain for drugs), urine (most useful for drug screening — qualitative), faeces, hair and nails (chronic poisoning — arsenic, thallium, drugs), and their chemical analysis (toxicological screening — spot tests, TLC, immunoassays, GC-MS, LC-MS/MS, AAS for metals); biochemical — serum cholinesterase (OP), ABG (metabolic acidosis — methanol, salicylates), anion gap and osmolal gap (methanol, ethylene glycol), carboxyhaemoglobin, methaemoglobin, liver and renal function tests, ECG (QRS widening in TCA, arrhythmias in aluminium phosphide, digoxin, oleander); (D) observation — clinical course, response to antidotes (naloxone in opioids, atropine challenge in OP), serial observations; secret watching in suspected homicidal poisoning (repeated symptoms when food given by suspect) and in feigned illness; (E) in homicidal poisoning suspect, the doctor should inform police and preserve samples. Diagnosis of poisoning in the dead (post-mortem): (A) external examination — colour of post-mortem staining (cherry-red in CO, bright pink in cyanide, chocolate-brown in nitrites/nitrobenzene, dark in asphyxiants), smell from body/mouth (cyanide, kerosene, phenol, alcohol, OP), stains and corrosion around mouth, lips, chin, hands, clothes (acids — sulphuric: black; nitric: yellow; carbolic: white turning brown; alkalis: soapy), froth at mouth (OP, opium), condition of pupils (unreliable after death), injection marks (drug abuse, insulin), jaundice, rigor mortis (early and marked in strychnine), decomposition (delayed in arsenic, antimony, chronic poisoning), skin lesions (arsenical pigmentation, keratosis, Mees' lines in nails — arsenic, thallium), hair loss (thallium); (B) internal examination — (1) smell on opening the body (especially cranial and stomach) — cyanide (bitter almonds), OP (kerosene-like), aluminium phosphide (garlic/decaying fish — phosphine), phenol, alcohol; (2) gastrointestinal tract — mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach and intestines for corrosion (blackened, charred mucosa with sulphuric acid; leathery white/grey with carbolic; slimy, swollen, soapy with alkalis), inflammation (congested, red mucosa — irritants), ulceration, haemorrhages, perforation, contents (pills, capsules, plant parts — seeds of datura, abrus, leaves of oleander; powder — arsenic white particles adhering to mucosa; colour of contents — blue-green with copper sulphate, yellow with nitric acid/picric acid; luminous in phosphorus), smell; (3) other organs — congestion and oedema of lungs and brain (CNS depressants, OP), fatty liver/necrosis (phosphorus, paracetamol, chloroform, carbon tetrachloride, mushrooms), acute tubular necrosis (mercury, ethylene glycol, oxalic acid — calcium oxalate crystals), myocardial lesions, subendocardial haemorrhages (arsenic), blood colour; (C) collection and preservation of viscera for chemical analysis (see topic 'preservation of viscera'), histopathology (liver, kidney, heart, lungs, brain), and microbiology; (D) negative findings do not exclude poisoning — many poisons (cyanide, opium, CNS depressants, insulin, succinylcholine, plant alkaloids) produce no specific gross changes; final opinion is reserved pending chemical analysis report; (E) circumstantial evidence — containers, residual material, suicide notes, scene examination, purchase records, motive, history of illness after eating food prepared by a particular person. Legal duties of the doctor in poisoning — see separate topic.",
[["Classification of poisons",[["Group","Subgroup","Examples"],["Corrosives","Mineral acids","H₂SO₄, HNO₃, HCl"],["","Organic acids","Carbolic, oxalic"],["","Alkalis","NaOH, KOH, NH₄OH"],["Irritants","Inorganic non-metallic","Phosphorus, iodine"],["","Inorganic metallic","Arsenic, lead, mercury, copper"],["","Organic vegetable","Abrus, castor, croton, calotropis"],["","Organic animal","Snake, scorpion, cantharides"],["","Mechanical","Powdered glass, diamond dust"],["Systemic — cerebral","Somniferous / inebriant / deliriant","Opium / alcohol, barbiturates / datura, cannabis, cocaine"],["Systemic — spinal","Convulsant","Strychnine"],["Systemic — peripheral","—","Curare, conium"],["Cardiac","—","Digitalis, oleander, aconite, tobacco"],["Asphyxiants","Gases","CO, CO₂, H₂S, cyanide"],["Miscellaneous","—","OP, phosphides, paracetamol, food poisoning"]]],
["Factors modifying action",["Dose","Form (gas > liquid > solid; solubility)","Route (IV > inhalation > IM > SC > oral)","Age, health, sleep, food in stomach","Idiosyncrasy, allergy","Tolerance, cumulative action","Synergism/antagonism"]],
["Odour clues",[["Odour","Poison"],["Garlic","Arsenic, phosphorus, OP, thallium"],["Bitter almonds","Cyanide"],["Rotten eggs","H₂S"],["Decaying fish/garlic","Aluminium phosphide (phosphine)"],["Kerosene","Kerosene, OP solvents"],["Phenolic","Carbolic acid"],["Shoe polish","Nitrobenzene"]]],
["Diagnosis",[["In living","In dead"],["History: sudden onset, after food, several affected","PM staining colour, smell"],["Toxidromes, odour, pupils, skin colour","Corrosion/inflammation of GIT; contents"],["Vomit, lavage fluid, blood, urine analysis","Organ changes (liver, kidney)"],["Biochemistry, ECG","Viscera preservation, chemical analysis"],["Observation, response to antidote","Histopathology; circumstantial evidence"]]]],
{c:"Poisons are classified as corrosives, irritants (inorganic, organic, mechanical) and systemic poisons acting on the brain, spinal cord, peripheral nerves, heart or oxygen transport, their effects modified by dose, form, route and host factors; diagnosis in the living relies on history, toxidromes and analysis of vomit, blood and urine, and in the dead on autopsy findings, preserved viscera and chemical analysis, remembering that negative autopsy does not exclude poisoning."});

Z("fmt8",1,"f",["In every case of suspected poisoning the doctor has medical duties — to save life by resuscitation, decontamination, antidotes and supportive care — and legal duties: to suspect and recognise poisoning, record history and findings, preserve vomit, lavage fluid, blood, urine and containers, inform the police (and the magistrate if the patient may die, so a dying declaration can be recorded), issue a death certificate only if satisfied of natural death and never in suspected poisoning, send viscera for chemical analysis after autopsy, and maintain records and confidentiality.","The doctor treats the patient first but also acts as the first investigator — what he saves, writes and reports may decide a criminal case.",["Medical duties: treat promptly (ABC, airway, oxygen, IV access), remove unabsorbed poison (gastric lavage within 1–2 h, activated charcoal, skin/eye decontamination), give antidotes, enhance elimination, supportive care, observe; treatment takes precedence over legal formalities.","Legal duties (in living): note history in patient's words; record symptoms, signs, treatment with time; collect and preserve vomit, first gastric washings, blood, urine, faeces, suspected material/containers — sealed, labelled, chain of custody; inform police (MLC) — S. 33 BNSS duty; in homicidal cases also protect patient (food given only through hospital), secret watching; if patient's condition critical — call magistrate for dying declaration (or record himself with witnesses).","If patient dies: do not issue death certificate (MCCD) in suspected poisoning; inform police for inquest and autopsy; hand over preserved material; doctor performing PM preserves viscera and sends to chemical examiner/FSL; give final opinion after report.","Private practitioner: legally bound to inform police in suspected homicidal poisoning (S. 33 BNSS / 39 CrPC lists offences; omission to inform punishable under S. 211 BNS); in suicidal/accidental — MLC also required; failure → offence (S. 211 BNS / 176 IPC); professional misconduct; doctor may be called as witness; consult senior colleague; maintain records."],"Treat first; preserve samples; inform police; never issue MCCD in suspected poisoning."],
"When a doctor is called to attend a case of suspected poisoning, he has both medical (therapeutic) and medicolegal duties. (I) Medical duties (primary — saving life): (1) immediate resuscitation and stabilisation — airway (clear secretions, position, intubation if GCS ≤ 8 or loss of protective reflexes), breathing (oxygen, ventilation), circulation (IV access, fluids, vasopressors), control of convulsions (benzodiazepines), correction of hypoglycaemia (dextrose), temperature, cardiac monitoring; (2) identification of the poison — history from patient, relatives and attendants (nature of poison, quantity, time, route, containers), examination for toxidromes, odour, pupils; (3) decontamination (removal of unabsorbed poison) — gastric lavage (within 1–2 hours of ingestion for life-threatening amounts; contraindicated in corrosives, hydrocarbons, convulsions, unprotected airway), activated charcoal (1 g/kg within 1 h — adsorbs most poisons except alcohols, acids, alkalis, metals, lithium, iron, hydrocarbons), whole bowel irrigation (iron, sustained-release drugs, body packers), skin decontamination (removing clothes, washing — OP, corrosives), eye irrigation; emetics (ipecac, salt water) — obsolete; (4) antidotes — specific (atropine and pralidoxime for OP; naloxone for opioids; flumazenil for benzodiazepines (cautious); N-acetylcysteine for paracetamol; hydroxocobalamin/sodium nitrite + sodium thiosulphate for cyanide; oxygen/hyperbaric oxygen for CO; fomepizole/ethanol for methanol; chelators — BAL, DMSA, CaNa₂EDTA, penicillamine, desferrioxamine for metals; ASV for snake bite; digoxin Fab for digitalis/oleander; vitamin K for warfarin; methylene blue for methaemoglobinaemia; physostigmine for anticholinergic; glucagon for β-blockers; calcium for calcium channel blockers/oxalic acid/hydrofluoric acid); non-specific (universal antidote — obsolete; activated charcoal); (5) enhanced elimination — multiple-dose activated charcoal, urinary alkalinisation (salicylates, phenobarbitone), haemodialysis/haemoperfusion (methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium, salicylates, theophylline); (6) symptomatic and supportive care — maintenance of fluid–electrolyte balance, nutrition, nursing care, treatment of complications (aspiration pneumonia, renal failure, hepatic failure, arrhythmias); (7) psychiatric evaluation in self-poisoning (suicide risk assessment) before discharge; (8) observation till out of danger. Treatment takes priority over legal formalities — a doctor cannot refuse or delay treatment on the ground that it is a medicolegal case (Parmanand Katara, 1989). (II) Medicolegal (legal) duties: (A) when the patient is alive — (1) suspect and recognise poisoning (doctor must not be misled by false history; be alert to homicidal poisoning — repeated unexplained illness, symptoms after food from a particular person); (2) record — a careful history (in the patient's own words, including names of persons suspected), time of onset of symptoms, time of consumption, food/drink taken, symptoms, signs, treatment given, times of each, progress; maintain records (MLC register, case sheet) safely; (3) collect and preserve evidence — vomit, first sample of stomach wash (lavage fluid — 'first washings' before adding chemicals), blood (10 ml in fluoride vial and plain/EDTA tube), urine (all passed — 30–50 ml), faeces, residual food/drink, containers, medicine strips, syringes, suicide note; seal, label (name, date, time, nature, signature) and hand over to police against receipt (chain of custody); if not available, store in a refrigerator with proper documentation; (4) inform the police (medicolegal case — intimation of all cases of poisoning — homicidal, suicidal, accidental) — under S. 33 BNSS (39 CrPC) every person aware of the commission of certain offences (including murder, attempt, causing hurt by poison — S. 123 BNS) shall forthwith give information to the nearest magistrate or police officer; omission to give information (S. 211 BNS / 176 IPC) is punishable; giving false information (S. 212 BNS / 177 IPC) is punishable; for private practitioners, the duty is the same; (5) if the patient's condition is serious and he may die, inform the magistrate to record a dying declaration; if there is no time, the doctor should record it himself in the presence of two witnesses after certifying that the patient is fit; (6) in suspected homicidal poisoning — inform police; protect the patient — ensure food and medicines are given only by trusted staff (hospital diet), keep a watch on visitors and suspected persons (secret watching), shift to hospital; inform a responsible relative of the suspicion (but with caution); consult a colleague (to share responsibility); do not reveal suspicion to the suspected person; (7) confidentiality — share information only with appropriate authorities; (8) issue certificates and reports when requested by police/court; (B) when the patient dies — (1) the doctor should not issue a death certificate (MCCD) if there is any suspicion of poisoning; he must inform the police so that inquest and medicolegal autopsy can be performed; (2) hand over all preserved materials, records and a report to the police; (3) at autopsy (by the autopsy surgeon) — note findings, preserve viscera and other samples with preservatives, sealed and labelled, along with a sample of the preservative and covering letter (requisition, brief history, findings, poison suspected), send through police to the Chemical Examiner/FSL; keep a portion of samples; the cause of death is reserved till the chemical analysis report is received; then the final opinion is given correlating the clinical history, autopsy findings and chemical analysis; (4) attend court and give evidence; (C) for private practitioners — the same legal duties; if the case is managed at home, advise hospitalisation; refer with records; in homicidal suspicion, it is a legal duty to inform police (not merely ethical); the doctor may be held liable for negligence if he fails to diagnose or treat appropriately, or for abetment/omission if he conceals; (D) industrial/occupational and mass poisoning — notify health authorities (food poisoning outbreaks — IDSP); (E) adverse drug reactions — report to Pharmacovigilance Programme of India.",
[["Medical duties",["Resuscitate (ABC), stabilise","Identify poison (history, toxidrome)","Decontaminate (lavage, charcoal, skin/eye)","Specific antidotes","Enhance elimination (MDAC, alkalinisation, dialysis)","Supportive care, treat complications","Psychiatric evaluation in self-harm"]],
["Legal duties — patient alive",[["Duty","Details"],["Record","History in patient's words, times, symptoms, treatment"],["Preserve","Vomit, first lavage fluid, blood, urine, faeces, containers — sealed, labelled"],["Inform police","MLC; S. 33 BNSS; omission punishable (S. 211 BNS)"],["Dying declaration","Call magistrate; or record with witnesses"],["Homicidal suspicion","Protect patient, secret watching, consult colleague"],["Confidentiality","Only to authorities"]]],
["Legal duties — patient dies",["No death certificate (MCCD)","Inform police for inquest and autopsy","Hand over preserved samples and records","Viscera preserved and sent to chemical examiner/FSL","Final opinion after chemical analysis","Attend court"]]],
{c:"In suspected poisoning the doctor must first save life through resuscitation, decontamination, antidotes and supportive care, while simultaneously recording the history, preserving vomit, lavage fluid, blood, urine and containers under chain of custody, informing the police, arranging a dying declaration if needed, protecting the patient in homicidal cases, and, if the patient dies, refusing a death certificate and ensuring autopsy with viscera sent for chemical analysis."});

Z("fmt8",2,"f",["Gastric lavage (stomach wash) is the removal of unabsorbed poison from the stomach by repeated instillation and aspiration of fluid through a wide-bore orogastric tube (Ewald/Boas 36–40 Fr in adults) with the patient in left lateral head-down position; indicated within 1–2 hours of ingestion of a life-threatening amount of poison (longer for drugs that delay gastric emptying — opioids, anticholinergics, salicylates), it is contraindicated in corrosive poisoning (except carbolic acid), hydrocarbons/kerosene (aspiration risk), convulsions or convulsants (strychnine) unless controlled, unprotected airway in coma, and bleeding disorders/oesophageal varices.","Lavage rinses poison out of the stomach before it is absorbed — useful early, dangerous if the gut is burnt (corrosives) or the airway is unprotected (aspiration).",["Indications: ingestion of significant non-corrosive poison within 1–2 h (e.g., OP compounds; activated charcoal preferred for most drugs), up to 4–6 h or more for poisons delaying emptying (opium, datura/anticholinergics, salicylates, TCAs, phenobarbitone) or forming concretions; also for collecting samples for chemical analysis (first washings preserved).","Contraindications: absolute — corrosives (acids, alkalis — perforation risk) except carbolic acid (phenol — lavage with olive oil/ warm water carefully as it hardens/anaesthetises mucosa), hydrocarbons (kerosene, petrol — chemical pneumonitis), unprotected airway (coma, absent gag — intubate first), convulsions (control first; strychnine); relative — oesophageal varices, recent GI surgery, bleeding diathesis, severe hypothermia, hypotension, uncooperative patient, very late presentation.","Procedure: consent (if possible), explain; left lateral, head down 15–20° (Trendelenburg), knees flexed; mouth gag; lubricated tube measured (mouth to xiphisternum ~50 cm), pass orally, confirm position (aspirate, air insufflation); aspirate stomach contents first (preserve); instil 200–300 ml (adults; 10 ml/kg children) warm water or normal saline (children — saline to avoid hyponatraemia), siphon out; repeat till returns clear (2–5 L); add specific agents — activated charcoal at end, potassium permanganate 1:5000–10,000 (alkaloids — opium, strychnine), tannic acid (alkaloids), sodium bicarbonate (iron), calcium gluconate (oxalic acid, fluoride), starch (iodine), desferrioxamine (iron).","Complications: aspiration pneumonia (most common), laryngospasm, hypoxia, oesophageal/gastric perforation, bleeding, arrhythmias, hypothermia, fluid/electrolyte imbalance (water intoxication in children), pushing poison into duodenum; current position (AACT/EAPCCT) — not routine; consider only in life-threatening ingestion within 1 h."],"Lavage: within 1–2 h; left lateral head-down; contraindicated in corrosives (not carbolic) and kerosene."],
"Gastric lavage (stomach wash, gastric irrigation) is the process of washing out the stomach to remove ingested poison that has not yet been absorbed; it is also a medicolegal procedure since the first portion of aspirate is preserved for chemical analysis. Indications: (1) ingestion of a potentially life-threatening amount of a non-corrosive poison when the procedure can be undertaken within 1 hour (classical teaching — up to 2–3 hours; traditional Indian teaching allows lavage up to 6 hours and longer); (2) the time limit may be extended in poisoning with substances that delay gastric emptying or reduce gut motility or form concretions/bezoars — opium and opioids, anticholinergics (datura, atropine, antihistamines, tricyclic antidepressants), salicylates (pylorospasm, concretions), phenobarbitone, phenothiazines, iron, meprobamate, sustained-release preparations — and in organophosphate poisoning (widely practised in India even beyond 1 h because of high toxicity and large amounts ingested); (3) poisons not adsorbed by activated charcoal (iron, lithium, heavy metals) if large amounts recently ingested; (4) to obtain samples for diagnosis and chemical analysis (medicolegal purpose); (5) in unconscious patients after airway protection by cuffed endotracheal intubation. Contraindications: (A) absolute — (1) corrosive poisoning (strong acids and alkalis) — risk of perforation of the softened oesophagus/stomach and spread of corrosive to the mediastinum/peritoneum; exception: carbolic acid (phenol) — lavage is permitted (and recommended) because phenol coagulates and hardens the gastric mucosa (making perforation unlikely) and anaesthetises it; lavage done carefully with lukewarm water containing olive oil, castor oil or glycerine (which dissolve phenol and delay absorption) or with charcoal; (2) hydrocarbons/volatile liquids (kerosene, petrol, turpentine) — high risk of aspiration and chemical pneumonitis (unless mixed with highly toxic substances like OP compounds, when lavage with a cuffed endotracheal tube in place may be considered); (3) unprotected airway — comatose patients with depressed gag reflex (unless intubated with a cuffed tube); (4) convulsions or convulsant poisons (strychnine — may precipitate convulsions; lavage only after controlling convulsions/under anaesthesia); (B) relative — oesophageal varices, oesophageal strictures, recent upper GI surgery, haemorrhagic diathesis/anticoagulation, severe hypothermia (risk of arrhythmias), haemodynamic instability (shock), uncooperative/agitated patient (risk of injury), very small children (risk of fluid overload), ingestion of non-toxic substances or non-toxic amounts, late presentation beyond the useful window, small pills/tablets already passed, and pregnancy (late — aspiration). Procedure: (1) explain to patient/attendants; obtain consent where possible (in medicolegal cases document; emergency treatment can proceed without consent); (2) equipment — wide-bore orogastric tube (Ewald or Boas tube; 36–40 French gauge — internal diameter ~ 1 cm in adults; 22–28 F in children — to allow tablet fragments to pass) with a funnel (siphon) or syringe; Ryle's tube (nasogastric, 16–18 F — too narrow for tablets; used only for liquid poisons/small children — less effective); mouth gag/bite block, lubricant (jelly), suction apparatus, lavage fluid (warm tap water or normal saline at 37 °C), specific antidotes, containers for samples, oxygen and resuscitation equipment; (3) position — patient placed in the left lateral position with head down (15–20° Trendelenburg) and knees flexed — this keeps the pylorus higher than the stomach body (reducing passage of contents into duodenum) and allows vomitus to drain out of the mouth (reducing aspiration); (4) remove dentures; insert mouth gag; measure the tube length (from bridge of nose/mouth to xiphisternum — usually ~ 50 cm mark at incisors); lubricate and pass the tube gently through the mouth over the tongue to the pharynx while asking the patient to swallow; advance into the stomach; confirm position (aspiration of gastric contents, testing pH, epigastric auscultation on air insufflation; patient able to speak — tube not in trachea; X-ray if doubtful); (5) aspirate the stomach contents first (before instilling any fluid) — preserve this first aspirate (first washings) in a clean labelled container for chemical analysis (medicolegal requirement); (6) instil 200–300 ml (adults; children 10–15 ml/kg, max 250 ml) of warm water (or normal saline — preferred in children to prevent hyponatraemia/water intoxication) through the funnel raised above the patient, then lower the funnel below the stomach level to siphon out the fluid (or aspirate with syringe); repeat the process until the return fluid is clear and free of particles and odour (usually 2–5 litres; up to 10 L in some protocols); avoid instilling large volumes (forces contents into duodenum); (7) at the end, specific antidotes or activated charcoal (50 g in adults; 1 g/kg in children) may be instilled and left in the stomach; pinch/clamp the tube while withdrawing to prevent aspiration of fluid from the tube; (8) monitor vitals, oxygen saturation; record quantity and character of washings; preserve samples, label, seal and hand over to police. Lavage fluids and additives (traditional): plain warm water or normal saline (most common); potassium permanganate solution 1:5000 to 1:10,000 (oxidising agent — oxidises alkaloids such as opium, morphine, strychnine, nicotine, phosphorus; must be fully dissolved — crystals are corrosive); tannic acid 4% (or strong tea) — precipitates alkaloids and metals; sodium bicarbonate 5% (iron — forms ferrous carbonate); desferrioxamine in bicarbonate (iron); calcium gluconate/lactate (oxalic acid — forms insoluble calcium oxalate; fluoride); starch solution (iodine); sodium thiosulphate (cyanide, iodine); sodium or magnesium sulphate (barium salts — forms insoluble barium sulphate); egg albumin/milk (mercuric chloride and heavy metals — protein binding); olive oil/vegetable oil/glycerine (phenol); activated charcoal slurry (general adsorbent). Complications: (1) aspiration of gastric contents into lungs (aspiration pneumonia — most common and serious), especially in unconscious patients without airway protection; (2) laryngospasm, hypoxia, cardiac arrhythmias (vagal stimulation — bradycardia), hypothermia (with cold fluids); (3) mechanical injury — oesophageal or gastric perforation, mucosal tears, bleeding, epistaxis (nasogastric tubes), insertion into trachea; (4) fluid and electrolyte imbalance — water intoxication and hyponatraemia (children), hypernatraemia (with saline in excessive volumes); (5) propulsion of poison into the small intestine (enhancing absorption) if large volumes used or wrong position; (6) delays other treatments (charcoal, antidotes); (7) psychological trauma, injuries in struggling patients. Current evidence and guidelines (AACT/EAPCCT position paper, 2004/2013): gastric lavage should not be employed routinely; it may be considered only for ingestion of a potentially life-threatening amount of poison within 60 minutes, when benefits outweigh risks; activated charcoal is preferred for most poisons; in India, gastric lavage remains commonly practised (OP and other pesticide poisoning) — studies show limited benefit and potential harm; decision individualised. Medicolegal: the first lavage aspirate is crucial evidence; record time, volume, colour, smell, presence of tablets; preserve and send for chemical analysis.",
[["Indications",["Life-threatening non-corrosive poison within 1 h (traditionally up to 2–6 h)","Longer for opioids, anticholinergics, salicylates, TCAs, phenobarbitone, OP","Poisons not adsorbed by charcoal (iron, lithium)","Collection of samples for chemical analysis"]],
["Contraindications",[["Absolute","Relative"],["Corrosives — acids, alkalis (except carbolic acid)","Oesophageal varices/stricture"],["Hydrocarbons (kerosene, petrol)","Bleeding diathesis"],["Unprotected airway (coma without intubation)","Recent GI surgery"],["Convulsions/convulsants (strychnine) until controlled","Hypothermia, shock"],["—","Uncooperative patient, late presentation"]]],
["Procedure summary",["Left lateral, head-down 15–20°, knees flexed","Ewald/Boas tube 36–40 F (adult)","Confirm position","Aspirate first; preserve first washings","200–300 ml warm water/saline per cycle (children 10–15 ml/kg, saline)","Repeat till clear (2–5 L)","Charcoal/antidote at end; clamp tube on withdrawal"]],
["Lavage additives",[["Agent","Poison"],["Potassium permanganate 1:5000–10,000","Alkaloids (opium, strychnine), phosphorus"],["Tannic acid","Alkaloids, metals"],["Sodium bicarbonate/desferrioxamine","Iron"],["Calcium gluconate","Oxalic acid, fluoride"],["Starch","Iodine"],["Egg albumin/milk","Mercury, heavy metals"],["Olive oil/glycerine","Carbolic acid"],["Activated charcoal","Most organic poisons"]]],
["Complications",["Aspiration pneumonia (commonest)","Laryngospasm, hypoxia, arrhythmias","Perforation, bleeding","Water intoxication/hyponatraemia (children)","Propulsion of poison into duodenum"]]],
{c:"Gastric lavage removes unabsorbed poison through a wide-bore orogastric tube with the patient in left lateral head-down position, ideally within an hour (longer for gastric-emptying-delaying poisons), with first washings preserved for analysis; it is contraindicated in corrosive ingestion except carbolic acid, in hydrocarbon poisoning, with an unprotected airway and with uncontrolled convulsions, and its main hazard is aspiration, so it is no longer routine."});

Z("fmt8",3,"f",["Antidotes are substances that counteract the effects of a poison; they are mechanical/physical (activated charcoal adsorbs, demulcents coat, bulky food), chemical (neutralise or form insoluble/inert compounds — e.g., calcium for oxalate, chelating agents BAL/EDTA/desferrioxamine for metals, Prussian blue for thallium), physiological/pharmacological (opposite action — atropine for OP, naloxone for opioids, N-acetylcysteine for paracetamol, flumazenil for benzodiazepines, oxygen for CO, nitrites/thiosulphate/hydroxocobalamin for cyanide, fomepizole/ethanol for methanol) and immunological (antivenom, digoxin Fab); the 'universal antidote' (2 parts burnt toast/activated charcoal, 1 part magnesium oxide, 1 part tannic acid) is obsolete.","An antidote either soaks the poison up, locks it chemically, or blocks its effect at the target — 'universal antidote' sounded good but works poorly.",["Universal antidote (historical): activated charcoal 2 parts (adsorbs alkaloids), magnesium oxide 1 part (neutralises acids), tannic acid 1 part (precipitates alkaloids, metals); dose 15 g in water; ineffective (tannic acid and charcoal interfere, tannic acid hepatotoxic) — replaced by activated charcoal alone.","Activated charcoal: 1 g/kg (adult 50 g) within 1 h; multiple doses for carbamazepine, dapsone, phenobarbitone, quinine, theophylline; not effective for alcohols, acids, alkalis, metals (iron, lithium), cyanide (weak), hydrocarbons.","Chemical antidotes: chelators — BAL (dimercaprol — arsenic, mercury, lead with EDTA, gold), CaNa₂EDTA (lead), DMSA/succimer (lead, arsenic, mercury), penicillamine (copper, lead), desferrioxamine (iron, aluminium), Prussian blue (thallium, caesium); calcium salts (oxalic acid, fluoride, HF, Ca-channel blockers); sodium thiosulphate (cyanide, iodine); potassium permanganate (oxidises alkaloids).","Physiological/pharmacological: atropine + pralidoxime (OP), naloxone (opioids), flumazenil (benzodiazepines), N-acetylcysteine (paracetamol), physostigmine (anticholinergics/datura), neostigmine (curare), fomepizole/ethanol (methanol, ethylene glycol), oxygen/HBO (CO), amyl nitrite → sodium nitrite → sodium thiosulphate or hydroxocobalamin (cyanide), methylene blue (methaemoglobinaemia — nitrites, dapsone, aniline), vitamin K (warfarin/superwarfarins), protamine (heparin), glucagon (β-blockers), octreotide (sulphonylureas), sodium bicarbonate (TCAs), digoxin Fab (digoxin, oleander), pyridoxine (isoniazid), dantrolene (malignant hyperthermia), cyproheptadine (serotonin syndrome), lipid emulsion (local anaesthetics).","Immunological: anti-snake venom (polyvalent — cobra, krait, Russell's viper, saw-scaled viper), scorpion antivenom (prazosin preferred in India), botulinum antitoxin, digoxin-specific Fab, anti-rabies immunoglobulin."],"Universal antidote = 2 charcoal : 1 MgO : 1 tannic acid — obsolete."],
"An antidote is a substance that counteracts or neutralises the action of a poison; antidotes are classified as: (I) mechanical (physical) antidotes — prevent absorption of poison by physical means without changing the chemical nature: (1) adsorbents — activated charcoal (fine black powder produced by pyrolysis of organic material, then activated by steam/oxidising gases to increase surface area to ~ 1000–2000 m²/g; adsorbs most organic and inorganic poisons by forming a complex — alkaloids (opium, strychnine), drugs (barbiturates, TCAs, salicylates, paracetamol, digoxin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, theophylline), OP compounds, plant poisons; dose — 1 g/kg (adults 50–100 g; children 1 g/kg) as slurry in water, orally or via tube, preferably within 1 hour of ingestion; multiple-dose activated charcoal (MDAC — 25–50 g every 4 h) enhances elimination by interrupting enterohepatic/enteroenteric circulation ('gut dialysis') for carbamazepine, dapsone, phenobarbitone, quinine, theophylline; poorly adsorbs alcohols (ethanol, methanol, ethylene glycol), strong acids and alkalis (and obscures endoscopy), metals (iron, lithium, lead, mercury, arsenic), cyanide (weak), hydrocarbons, potassium, fluoride; complications — vomiting, aspiration (especially with depressed consciousness without airway protection), constipation, bowel obstruction; contraindicated in corrosives, hydrocarbons, unprotected airway, GI obstruction/perforation); (2) demulcents — soothing agents that form a protective coat over the mucosa and delay absorption of irritants and corrosives — milk, egg white (albumin), starch, gum arabic, oils (olive oil — except in phosphorus and cantharides poisoning where fats increase absorption), glycerine; (3) bulky food — e.g., bananas, mashed potatoes in poisoning by powdered glass (embed sharp particles); (4) diluents — water, milk (in corrosives — dilution); (5) emollients. (II) chemical antidotes — react chemically with the poison to form harmless or insoluble compounds: (1) neutralisation — weak acids (vinegar, lemon juice) for alkalis, and weak alkalis (magnesium oxide/milk of magnesia, chalk, soap solution) for acids — but now discouraged (exothermic reaction, gas formation) — dilution with milk/water preferred; avoid bicarbonates (CO₂ — distension, perforation); (2) precipitation — tannic acid (precipitates alkaloids, glycosides and metals); calcium salts (calcium gluconate/lactate) precipitate oxalic acid as insoluble calcium oxalate and fluoride as calcium fluoride; sodium/magnesium sulphate precipitate barium (and lead) salts as insoluble sulphates; common salt (sodium chloride) precipitates silver nitrate as silver chloride; starch for iodine (forms blue starch iodide); egg albumin/milk proteins for mercuric chloride and other heavy metals (protein binding); (3) oxidation — potassium permanganate (1:5000–1:10,000) oxidises alkaloids (morphine, strychnine, nicotine), phosphorus, cyanide; (4) chelating agents (form stable, water-soluble, non-toxic complexes with metals that are excreted in urine) — British Anti-Lewisite (BAL, dimercaprol; 2,3-dimercaptopropanol — developed against arsenical war gas lewisite; IM in arachis oil; for arsenic, mercury (inorganic), gold, antimony, bismuth, lead (with CaNa₂EDTA in encephalopathy); contraindicated in iron, cadmium, selenium (toxic complexes), G6PD deficiency (haemolysis)); calcium disodium EDTA (CaNa₂EDTA — lead; not Na₂EDTA which causes hypocalcaemia); DMSA (succimer, meso-2,3-dimercaptosuccinic acid — oral; lead (children), arsenic, mercury); DMPS (unithiol — arsenic, mercury); D-penicillamine (copper — Wilson's disease, lead, mercury, arsenic, gold); desferrioxamine (deferoxamine — iron, aluminium; iron complex — ferrioxamine gives 'vin rosé' urine); deferiprone, deferasirox (chronic iron overload); Prussian blue (potassium ferric hexacyanoferrate — thallium and radioactive caesium — interrupts enterohepatic circulation); trientine (copper); sodium calcium edetate; (5) other chemical antidotes — sodium thiosulphate (cyanide → thiocyanate via rhodanese; also iodine, bromate); hydroxocobalamin (cyanide → cyanocobalamin); dicobalt edetate (cyanide); sodium nitrite/amyl nitrite (form methaemoglobin which binds cyanide → cyanmethaemoglobin); protamine sulphate (heparin — ionic binding); sugammadex (rocuronium); (III) physiological (pharmacological/functional) antidotes — act on the tissues/receptors and produce effects opposite to those of the poison, or competitively block its action, or enhance its detoxification: (1) receptor antagonists — atropine (muscarinic antagonist — blocks the effects of acetylcholine in OP and carbamate poisoning; also for muscarinic mushroom poisoning); naloxone (opioid receptor antagonist — opioids, morphine, heroin, codeine, tramadol partial, Lomotil, clonidine partially); nalmefene; flumazenil (benzodiazepine receptor antagonist — benzodiazepines, zolpidem; caution — seizures in mixed overdose with TCA or in dependents); (2) enzyme reactivators — pralidoxime (2-PAM) and obidoxime (cholinesterase reactivators — OP poisoning, before 'ageing'); (3) cholinesterase inhibitors — physostigmine (anticholinergic poisoning — datura, atropine; crosses BBB), neostigmine/edrophonium (curare-like neuromuscular blockers, cobra envenomation adjunct); (4) replenishing glutathione — N-acetylcysteine (paracetamol poisoning — precursor of glutathione; also mushroom (Amanita), CCl₄, chloroform); (5) inhibiting toxic metabolism — fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole) and ethanol (alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitors — prevent conversion of methanol to formaldehyde/formic acid and ethylene glycol to glycolate/oxalate); folinic acid/folic acid (methanol — enhances formate metabolism); (6) oxygen — carbon monoxide (100% normobaric oxygen reduces COHb half-life from ~ 4–5 h to ~ 60–90 min; hyperbaric oxygen (2.5–3 ATA) to ~ 20–30 min), cyanide, H₂S, methaemoglobinaemia (adjunct); (7) methylene blue (1–2 mg/kg IV — reduces methaemoglobin to haemoglobin via NADPH methaemoglobin reductase — nitrites, nitrates, aniline, nitrobenzene, dapsone, local anaesthetics, primaquine; contraindicated in G6PD deficiency); (8) vitamin K₁ (phytomenadione — warfarin and superwarfarin (rodenticide) poisoning); (9) glucagon (β-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose — bypasses receptor to increase cAMP); high-dose insulin euglycaemia therapy; calcium (calcium channel blockers, hypocalcaemia from oxalic acid, fluoride, HF, ethylene glycol, magnesium toxicity); (10) sodium bicarbonate (TCA overdose — QRS widening; salicylates and phenobarbitone — urinary alkalinisation; methanol acidosis); (11) pyridoxine (vitamin B₆ — isoniazid-induced seizures; also Gyromitra mushroom, hydrazine, ethylene glycol adjunct); thiamine (ethylene glycol, Wernicke's in alcoholics); (12) octreotide (sulphonylurea-induced hypoglycaemia), dextrose (insulin, sulphonylureas); (13) dantrolene (malignant hyperthermia, NMS), bromocriptine (NMS), cyproheptadine (serotonin syndrome); (14) benzodiazepines (convulsants — strychnine, cocaine, amphetamines, organophosphates, chloroquine toxicity), barbiturates; (15) intravenous lipid emulsion (local anaesthetic systemic toxicity — bupivacaine; lipophilic drugs); (16) hydroxocobalamin (cyanide); (17) L-carnitine (valproate hepatotoxicity), uridine triacetate (5-FU), leucovorin (methotrexate), glucarpidase; (18) prazosin (scorpion sting — α-blockade for autonomic storm; drug of choice in India — 'Bawaskar'); (IV) immunological antidotes — antibodies or antibody fragments that bind and neutralise the poison: polyvalent anti-snake venom (ASV — equine F(ab')₂ antibodies against the 'big four' Indian snakes: cobra, common krait, Russell's viper, saw-scaled viper), monovalent antivenoms, scorpion antivenom, antivenoms for spiders, jellyfish; digoxin-specific antibody fragments (DigiFab — digoxin, digitoxin, oleander and other cardiac glycoside poisoning); botulinum antitoxin; tetanus immunoglobulin; anti-rabies immunoglobulin; idarucizumab (dabigatran). Universal antidote: a historical mixture proposed for use when the nature of the poison was unknown — composition: powdered burnt toast/activated charcoal 2 parts (adsorbs alkaloids and other poisons), magnesium oxide 1 part (neutralises acids — weak alkali), tannic acid 1 part (precipitates alkaloids, glycosides and metals); dose — 15 g (1 tablespoon) in half a glass of warm water; it is now considered obsolete and ineffective: tannic acid and magnesium oxide reduce the adsorptive capacity of charcoal (they are themselves adsorbed), tannic acid is hepatotoxic, burnt toast is not activated charcoal, and the components neutralise each other; activated charcoal alone is far superior; 'strong tea' was used as a source of tannic acid. Principles of use: antidotes are available for relatively few poisons; supportive care remains the mainstay; antidote should be given in the right dose by the right route at the right time; many antidotes have their own toxicity (e.g., nitrites cause hypotension and methaemoglobinaemia; BAL — hypertension, tachycardia; flumazenil — seizures; physostigmine — bradycardia, asystole in TCA toxicity; naloxone — acute withdrawal in dependents).",
[["Types of antidotes",[["Type","Mechanism","Examples"],["Mechanical/physical","Adsorb, coat, dilute","Activated charcoal, demulcents (milk, egg white), bulky food (glass)"],["Chemical","Neutralise, precipitate, oxidise, chelate","Calcium (oxalate), KMnO₄ (alkaloids), BAL, EDTA, desferrioxamine, Prussian blue"],["Physiological","Opposite action, receptor block, enzyme reactivation","Atropine, pralidoxime, naloxone, NAC, fomepizole, oxygen, methylene blue"],["Immunological","Antibody binding","ASV, digoxin Fab, botulinum antitoxin"]]],
["Universal antidote (obsolete)",[["Component","Parts","Purpose"],["Activated charcoal (burnt toast)","2","Adsorbs alkaloids"],["Magnesium oxide","1","Neutralises acids"],["Tannic acid","1","Precipitates alkaloids, metals"],["Why obsolete","—","Components interfere; tannic acid hepatotoxic; charcoal alone superior"]]],
["Specific antidotes",[["Poison","Antidote"],["Organophosphates","Atropine + pralidoxime"],["Opioids","Naloxone"],["Benzodiazepines","Flumazenil"],["Paracetamol","N-acetylcysteine"],["Methanol/ethylene glycol","Fomepizole/ethanol (+ folinic acid, dialysis)"],["Carbon monoxide","100% O₂ / HBO"],["Cyanide","Hydroxocobalamin; nitrites + thiosulphate"],["Methaemoglobinaemia","Methylene blue"],["Anticholinergics (datura)","Physostigmine"],["Warfarin/superwarfarin","Vitamin K₁"],["Heparin","Protamine"],["Iron","Desferrioxamine"],["Lead","CaNa₂EDTA, DMSA, BAL"],["Arsenic, mercury","BAL, DMSA, DMPS"],["Copper","Penicillamine"],["Thallium","Prussian blue"],["Digoxin/oleander","Digoxin Fab"],["β-blockers","Glucagon"],["TCAs","Sodium bicarbonate"],["Isoniazid","Pyridoxine"],["Oxalic acid","Calcium gluconate"],["Snake bite","Polyvalent ASV"],["Scorpion","Prazosin"]]],
["Activated charcoal",["Dose 1 g/kg (adult 50 g) within 1 h","MDAC: carbamazepine, dapsone, phenobarbitone, quinine, theophylline","Ineffective: alcohols, acids, alkalis, metals, lithium, hydrocarbons, cyanide (weak)","Contraindicated: corrosives, hydrocarbons, unprotected airway, GI obstruction"]]],
{c:"Antidotes counteract poisons mechanically (activated charcoal, demulcents), chemically (neutralisers, precipitants, oxidisers and chelators like BAL, EDTA and desferrioxamine), physiologically (atropine with pralidoxime, naloxone, N-acetylcysteine, fomepizole, oxygen, methylene blue, nitrites and hydroxocobalamin) or immunologically (antivenoms, digoxin Fab); the old universal antidote of charcoal, magnesium oxide and tannic acid is obsolete, and activated charcoal with supportive care is the mainstay."});

Z("fmt8",4,"f",["Viscera and body fluids are preserved at autopsy in suspected poisoning for chemical analysis by the Chemical Examiner/FSL: routinely (1) stomach with contents, (2) proximal 30 cm small intestine with contents, (3) liver 500 g (with gallbladder), (4) half of each kidney, (5) blood 10–30 ml and (6) urine 30–100 ml, plus special samples according to the suspected poison (brain for volatile poisons, lungs for gases, spleen, bone/hair/nails for metals, vitreous, bile, CSF, skin at injection site, fat for pesticides); the usual preservative is saturated solution of common salt (sodium chloride), with rectified spirit for some, sodium fluoride for blood (alcohol, cyanide), and refrigeration without preservative for volatiles and modern analysis — never formalin for toxicology; each sample in a separate clean wide-mouthed glass jar, sealed, labelled, with a sample of preservative, and sent with the inquest/PM papers through police.","Preserving viscera freezes the evidence — the right organs, right container, right preservative, sealed with an unbroken chain of custody.",["Routine viscera: stomach + contents (jar 1), small intestine (30 cm) + contents (jar 1 or 2), liver (500 g) + kidney (half each) (jar 2/3) — in Indian practice 3 jars: (1) stomach + intestine, (2) liver + kidney (+ spleen), (3) blood (+ preservative sample); jars ~1 L, wide-mouthed, glass, clean.","Preservatives: saturated NaCl (common salt) — routine viscera; rectified spirit — not for alcohol, kerosene, phenol, phosphorus, paraldehyde, chloroform, formalin, acetic acid; sodium fluoride (10 mg/ml) + potassium oxalate (anticoagulant) — blood for alcohol, cyanide, CO; thymol/sodium fluoride — urine; 10% formalin — only histopathology (not toxicology); no preservative (refrigerate/ice) — when salt/spirit interferes; quantity — viscera covered with preservative, not filling > 2/3 of jar.","Special samples: brain (volatile — alcohol, chloroform, kerosene, cyanide; alkaloids), lungs (gases, volatile), heart blood (CO, digoxin), spinal cord (strychnine), vitreous humour (alcohol, electrolytes, digoxin), bile (opiates, chlorpromazine, glutethimide), hair and nails (chronic arsenic, thallium, mercury, drugs), long bone (lead, arsenic, radium), skin + muscle at injection site (insulin, snake venom, drugs), fat (organochlorines, thiopentone), uterus (abortifacients), maggots (decomposed).","Procedure: label (name, PM no., date, organ, preservative, signatures), seal with doctor's seal (sample seal impression on forwarding letter), forwarding letter/requisition with brief history, PM findings, suspected poison; sample of preservative (25 g salt / 25 ml spirit) separately; handed to police; chain of custody; avoid contamination (no metal containers, clean jars); deliver promptly (store in cold)."],"Routine: stomach, intestine, liver, kidney, blood, urine — in saturated salt; never formalin."],
"Chemical analysis of viscera and body fluids is essential to establish poisoning as the cause of death and to identify and quantify the poison; the autopsy surgeon is responsible for proper collection, preservation, sealing, labelling and dispatch to the Chemical Examiner (State Forensic Science Laboratory/Regional FSL); errors at this stage may result in failure to detect the poison or in contamination and wrong conclusions. Indications for preservation: (a) suspected or alleged poisoning (history, symptoms, circumstances, autopsy findings); (b) deaths where no cause is found at autopsy (negative/obscure autopsy); (c) deaths in custody, dowry deaths and other suspicious deaths (as per routine protocol); (d) deaths following drug/alcohol use, road accidents (alcohol/drugs), burns (CO, cyanide), drowning (alcohol/drugs), anaesthetic deaths, sudden deaths; (e) decomposed bodies; (f) exhumation. Routine viscera to be preserved (in adults): (1) stomach with its contents — the whole stomach is removed after tying both ends (cardiac and pyloric) and placed with its contents in a jar; contents may be collected separately if large (note smell, colour, quantity, tablets/foreign material); (2) small intestine with contents — the upper part (about 30 cm / 1 foot of duodenum and jejunum, where absorption occurs and poison is concentrated) — ligated; in some protocols the whole small intestine; (3) liver — about 500 g (or whole liver in children), preferably with gallbladder (bile); the liver is the main organ of metabolism and concentrates many poisons (alkaloids, barbiturates, metals, pesticides); (4) kidneys — one-half of each kidney (or one whole kidney) — organs of excretion (metals, drugs); (5) blood — 10–30 ml (from peripheral veins — femoral vein preferred over heart blood to avoid post-mortem redistribution; heart blood if not available), collected in two containers: one with preservative sodium fluoride (1–2%, 10 mg/ml) plus potassium oxalate/EDTA as anticoagulant (for alcohol, cyanide, CO, drugs), another without preservative (plain) for other analyses; (6) urine — 30–100 ml (all available) — from the bladder by syringe (urine contains high concentrations of many drugs and metabolites — excellent for screening); preservative — sodium fluoride/thymol or none (refrigerated); (7) in Indian practice, the routine viscera are usually sent in three jars: jar 1 — stomach and its contents + portion of small intestine and its contents; jar 2 — liver, spleen (part) and kidneys (half of each); jar 3 — blood (with preservative); urine separately; plus a sample of the preservative used (in a separate bottle). Special samples (according to the suspected poison): (1) brain — volatile and organic poisons (alcohol, chloroform, ether, kerosene, cyanide, hydrocarbons, strychnine, CNS depressants, alkaloids, organophosphates) — about 100 g; (2) lungs — gases and volatile poisons (CO, hydrocarbons, solvents, cyanide, inhaled drugs) — in airtight containers/nylon bags; (3) heart (with blood) — for digitalis/oleander and CO; (4) spinal cord — strychnine (historically); (5) vitreous humour — alcohol (correlates with blood alcohol, less affected by putrefaction), digoxin, electrolytes, glucose — collected with fine needle from each eye (2–3 ml); (6) bile — opiates (morphine concentrates in bile), chlorpromazine, glutethimide, barbiturates, cocaine; (7) CSF — alcohol, drugs; (8) hair (with roots — pulled, 50–100 mg, tied with thread at the root end for segmental analysis) and nails (clippings) — chronic poisoning with arsenic, thallium, mercury, lead, antimony; drug use history (opioids, cocaine, cannabis, benzodiazepines — months back); (9) bones (long bone — 10 cm of femur shaft) — chronic arsenic, lead, radium, fluoride; skeletal remains; (10) skin and underlying tissue/muscle around injection site (with control sample from the opposite side) — injected poisons (insulin, opiates, snake venom, succinylcholine, potassium chloride, local anaesthetics); skin at site of application of corrosives/OP; (11) subcutaneous fat/omental fat — fat-soluble poisons (organochlorine insecticides, thiopentone, hydrocarbons, chloroform); (12) uterus and vaginal swabs/contents — abortifacient pastes and pills, abortion sticks; (13) spleen — cyanide, barbiturates; (14) muscle — in decomposed bodies (drugs); (15) maggots/pupae — entomotoxicology in decomposed bodies; (16) stomach wall/intestine portions for metals; (17) samples of food, vomit, stomach wash collected during treatment, containers, syringes, tablets found on the body or at scene — sealed separately; (18) in exhumation — soil samples above, below, and both sides of the body plus a control; coffin wood, shroud; (19) in children — whole organs (small size). Preservatives: (1) saturated solution of common salt (sodium chloride) — the routine preservative for viscera in India; prepared by dissolving common salt in water till no more dissolves (excess crystals at the bottom); avoided when common salt (sodium chloride) poisoning itself is suspected; salt is cheap, readily available, does not interfere with most analyses; (2) rectified spirit (95% ethyl alcohol) — used in many poisons (alkaloids, OP, plant poisons) but must not be used in poisoning by alcohol (ethanol, methanol), chloral hydrate, paraldehyde, carbolic acid (phenol), phosphorus (dissolves it), kerosene and other hydrocarbons, formic/acetic acids, chloroform, ether — because these are volatile, alcohol-soluble or interfere with tests; in such cases saturated salt is used; (3) sodium fluoride (1–2% or 10 mg/ml blood) — for blood in suspected alcohol (inhibits glycolysis and microbial fermentation that can produce or destroy alcohol), cyanide, CO, cocaine (inhibits esterases); combined with potassium oxalate (anticoagulant; 3 mg/ml) — 'grey-top' vacutainer; (4) thymol (crystal) or sodium fluoride for urine; (5) 10% formalin — for histopathology samples only (organs/tissues for microscopic examination); never used for viscera intended for toxicology because formaldehyde reacts with and destroys many poisons and interferes with extraction and analysis (exception: in some laboratories, formalin-fixed tissue analysed for metals); (6) no preservative (refrigeration at 4 °C or freezing at −20 °C) — preferred for many modern analytical methods (GC-MS, LC-MS); used for gases/volatiles (lungs in airtight containers), for cases where both salt and spirit would interfere, and when samples reach the laboratory quickly. Containers: clean, dry, wide-mouthed, glass jars (about 1 litre capacity) with screw caps/glass stoppers, clean and free from contamination; not metal containers (react) or ordinary plastic (may absorb or leach); viscera should not fill more than two-thirds of the jar; preservative should be equal in volume and cover the viscera completely; for blood and urine — glass vials/vacutainers; for volatiles — airtight, filled completely to minimise headspace; for gases — nylon bags (lungs), glass vials. Procedure: (1) use clean instruments; avoid contamination (do not wash viscera with water — to avoid dilution/contamination — although external washing of contaminants may be required); (2) incise the stomach and intestines to allow preservative to penetrate (after noting contents); slice solid organs (liver, kidney) into small pieces (1–2 cm) for better penetration of the preservative; (3) label each jar — name of deceased, age, sex, PM number, date and time, name of police station and case number, viscera contained, preservative used; signature of the medical officer; (4) seal the jar — cover the mouth with cloth/paper, tie with thread and seal with the doctor's seal (sealing wax, at multiple points) so that it cannot be opened without breaking the seal; affix a specimen impression of the seal on the forwarding letter for verification; (5) a sample of the preservative used (about 25 g of salt or 25 ml of spirit) is sent separately in a sealed bottle as control (to exclude contamination from the preservative); (6) forwarding letter (requisition) to the Chemical Examiner — through the investigating police officer, containing the identification details, brief history (symptoms, treatment, time of death), relevant autopsy findings (smell, colour, corrosion), suspected poison, list of articles with seals, preservative used, and request for analysis; copy of the inquest report and PM report; (7) hand over to the police against receipt (chain of custody documentation); (8) the jars should be dispatched as early as possible (preferably within days); stored in a cool place till dispatch; (9) the final opinion on cause of death is deferred ('kept pending') until the chemical analysis report is received; the report is interpreted with clinical history and autopsy findings (therapeutic vs toxic vs fatal levels; post-mortem redistribution; putrefaction effects — production of alcohol, cyanide in small amounts; loss of volatile poisons; negative report does not exclude poisoning — poison may be metabolised, eliminated, vomited, washed out by lavage, lost due to improper preservation/delay, or below detection limits, or not tested for). Quantities (adult, approximate): stomach and contents — whole; small intestine — 30 cm; liver — 500 g; kidney — half of each; blood — 10–30 ml; urine — 30–100 ml; brain — 100 g; vitreous — all; hair — a pencil-thick lock; nails — all clippings; bone — 10 cm femur.",
[["Routine viscera",[["Sample","Quantity","Purpose"],["Stomach + contents","Whole","Unabsorbed poison"],["Small intestine + contents","30 cm (upper)","Poison in transit"],["Liver (+ gallbladder)","500 g","Metabolism, concentration"],["Kidney","Half of each","Excretion (metals, drugs)"],["Blood","10–30 ml (femoral)","Circulating level; NaF for alcohol/cyanide"],["Urine","30–100 ml","Screening"]]],
["Preservatives",[["Preservative","Use","Avoid in"],["Saturated NaCl","Routine viscera","Common salt poisoning"],["Rectified spirit","Alkaloids, OP, plant poisons","Alcohol, kerosene, phenol, phosphorus, paraldehyde, chloroform, formic/acetic acid"],["Sodium fluoride + oxalate","Blood (alcohol, cyanide, CO, cocaine)","—"],["Thymol/NaF","Urine","—"],["10% formalin","Histopathology only","All toxicology"],["None (refrigerate/freeze)","Gases, volatiles, modern analysis","—"]]],
["Special samples by poison",[["Sample","Poison"],["Brain","Volatile (alcohol, chloroform, kerosene), cyanide, alkaloids"],["Lungs (airtight)","Gases, volatile solvents"],["Vitreous humour","Alcohol, digoxin, electrolytes"],["Bile","Opiates, chlorpromazine, barbiturates"],["Hair, nails","Chronic arsenic, thallium, mercury; drug history"],["Long bone","Lead, arsenic, radium"],["Skin + muscle at injection site (+ control)","Insulin, snake venom, drugs"],["Fat","Organochlorines, thiopentone"],["Uterus","Abortifacients"],["Maggots","Decomposed bodies"]]],
["Procedure",["Clean wide-mouthed glass jars; ≤ 2/3 filled; covered with preservative","Slice solid organs; open hollow viscera","Label: name, PM no., date, organs, preservative, signature","Seal with doctor's seal; specimen seal on letter","Separate sample of preservative","Forwarding letter with history, findings, suspected poison","Hand to police (chain of custody); dispatch promptly","Opinion reserved till chemical report"]]],
{c:"In suspected poisoning, the stomach and contents, upper small intestine, 500 g liver, half of each kidney, blood and urine are routinely preserved in clean glass jars — viscera in saturated salt (or rectified spirit unless alcohol, kerosene, phenol or phosphorus are suspected), blood with sodium fluoride and never formalin — supplemented by brain, lungs, vitreous, bile, hair, nails, bone or injection-site tissue as the suspected poison dictates, all sealed, labelled, accompanied by a preservative sample and sent through police with an unbroken chain of custody."});

Z("fmt8",5,"f",["Enhanced elimination techniques increase removal of an absorbed poison from the body: forced diuresis with urinary pH manipulation (alkaline diuresis with sodium bicarbonate for salicylates, phenobarbitone, chlorpropamide, 2,4-D; forced neutral diuresis largely abandoned; acid diuresis obsolete), multiple-dose activated charcoal, and extracorporeal methods — haemodialysis (small, water-soluble, low protein-bound, low volume of distribution poisons: methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium, salicylates, theophylline, metformin, valproate), haemoperfusion (charcoal/resin — lipid-soluble/protein-bound: theophylline, barbiturates, paraquat), plus exchange transfusion and plasmapheresis in special cases.","Once the poison is in the blood, we can push it out through the kidneys (diuresis with pH trapping) or filter it out through a machine (dialysis, haemoperfusion).",["Forced alkaline diuresis: IV fluids with sodium bicarbonate to keep urine pH 7.5–8.5 → weak acids ionised in tubular fluid ('ion trapping') and not reabsorbed — salicylates, phenobarbitone, chlorpropamide, 2,4-D (chlorophenoxy herbicides), methotrexate; monitor K⁺ (hypokalaemia prevents alkalinisation), fluid overload; urinary alkalinisation without forced diuresis now preferred.","Forced acid diuresis (ammonium chloride, ascorbic acid — for weak bases: amphetamines, quinine, phencyclidine, fenfluramine) — abandoned (risk of rhabdomyolysis-related renal failure, acidosis).","Haemodialysis — criteria: low molecular weight (< 500 Da), water-soluble, low protein binding, small volume of distribution (< 1 L/kg), high toxicity; indications — methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium, salicylates (severe), theophylline, metformin (lactic acidosis), valproate, barbiturates (long-acting), isopropanol, toxic alcohols; also corrects acidosis, electrolytes, renal failure; peritoneal dialysis less efficient.","Haemoperfusion: blood passed through charcoal or resin cartridge — adsorbs lipid-soluble/protein-bound drugs (theophylline, phenobarbitone, carbamazepine, paraquat, Amanita toxins); complications — thrombocytopenia, hypocalcaemia, hypoglycaemia; others — CRRT, MARS (liver dialysis), exchange transfusion (neonates; methaemoglobinaemia, haemolysis), plasmapheresis (highly protein-bound — mushroom, thyroxine), MDAC ('gut dialysis')."],"Alkaline diuresis: salicylates, phenobarbitone; Haemodialysis: methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium."],
"Methods to enhance elimination of poisons are used after absorption, when the poison is present in toxic amounts in the circulation, when the natural elimination is slow or impaired (renal or hepatic failure), or when the clinical condition is severe or deteriorating despite supportive care; they include: (1) multiple-dose activated charcoal (MDAC); (2) forced diuresis and urinary pH manipulation; (3) extracorporeal techniques — haemodialysis, haemoperfusion, haemofiltration (continuous renal replacement therapy), peritoneal dialysis, plasmapheresis, exchange transfusion, molecular adsorbent recirculating system (MARS); (4) chelation therapy (for metals — increases renal excretion); (5) hyperbaric/normobaric oxygen (CO elimination via lungs). (I) Forced diuresis: the principle is that increasing urine flow (by IV fluids ± diuretics) reduces the time of contact of the poison with the renal tubular epithelium and reduces passive tubular reabsorption, thus increasing renal clearance; it is effective only for poisons excreted unchanged by the kidney, with small volume of distribution and low protein binding; forced diuresis alone (neutral) produces only modest increase and carries risks of fluid overload, pulmonary and cerebral oedema, electrolyte imbalance — it is now largely abandoned; manipulation of urinary pH is more important: ion trapping — non-ionised (lipid-soluble) drug molecules are reabsorbed across tubular cells, while ionised (water-soluble) forms are trapped in the tubular fluid and excreted; weak acids are ionised in alkaline urine, weak bases in acidic urine; (a) forced alkaline diuresis / urinary alkalinisation — IV sodium bicarbonate (e.g., 1–2 mEq/kg bolus then infusion of 150 mEq NaHCO₃ in 1 L of 5% dextrose at 1.5–2 times maintenance) to achieve urine pH 7.5–8.5; potassium supplementation is essential (hypokalaemia prevents alkalinisation because the kidney reabsorbs K⁺ in exchange for H⁺ — paradoxical aciduria); monitor serum K⁺, Na⁺, Ca²⁺ (hypocalcaemia — tetany), blood pH (avoid pH > 7.55), fluid balance; indications — salicylate poisoning (moderate–severe; the most important indication: increases salicylate clearance several-fold; also reduces CNS entry by alkalinising plasma), phenobarbitone (long-acting barbiturate; MDAC more effective), chlorpropamide, chlorophenoxy herbicides (2,4-D, mecoprop — high-flow alkaline diuresis), methotrexate (prevents crystalluria), diflunisal, fluoride, rhabdomyolysis (to prevent myoglobin precipitation — controversial); 'urine alkalinisation' (bicarbonate without forced fluids) is now the recommended term (AACT/EAPCCT 2004); contraindications — renal failure, pulmonary oedema, heart failure, cerebral oedema; complications — alkalaemia, hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia, fluid overload, hypernatraemia; (b) forced acid diuresis (ammonium chloride, arginine hydrochloride, ascorbic acid — urine pH 5.5–6.5) — formerly used for weak bases (amphetamines, phencyclidine, quinine, quinidine, fenfluramine) but abandoned because the increase in elimination is small, and it can precipitate myoglobin in renal tubules (rhabdomyolysis is common in amphetamine/PCP toxicity) causing acute renal failure, and causes systemic acidosis; (c) forced neutral diuresis (mannitol/frusemide with fluids) — historically for lithium and bromide — not recommended. (II) Extracorporeal elimination: (A) haemodialysis (HD) — blood is passed through a dialyser where toxins diffuse across a semipermeable membrane down a concentration gradient into the dialysate; properties of poisons that are effectively dialysed: (1) low molecular weight (< 500 daltons — up to ~ 15,000 Da with high-flux membranes), (2) water solubility, (3) low protein binding (< 80%), (4) small volume of distribution (< 1 L/kg — mostly in plasma), (5) single-compartment kinetics, (6) low endogenous clearance (otherwise dialysis adds little); in addition, haemodialysis corrects metabolic acidosis, electrolyte disturbances (hyperkalaemia), fluid overload and uraemia; indications (EXTRIP recommendations) — toxic alcohols: methanol (formic acid; with fomepizole/ethanol, folate — dialysis if methanol > 50 mg/dl, severe acidosis, visual disturbances, renal failure), ethylene glycol (glycolic and oxalic acids; dialysis for severe acidosis, renal failure, high levels), isopropanol (severe), ethanol (severe life-threatening); lithium (especially chronic toxicity with neurological features, renal impairment; levels > 4 mEq/L or > 2.5 with symptoms); salicylates (severe — level > 100 mg/dl acute or > 60 chronic, CNS features, pulmonary oedema, renal failure, refractory acidosis); theophylline (level > 100 mg/L acute, seizures, arrhythmias — HD or haemoperfusion); metformin-associated lactic acidosis (lactate > 20 mmol/L, pH < 7.0); valproic acid (> 1300 mg/L, coma); phenobarbitone and other long-acting barbiturates (prolonged coma); carbamazepine (high-flux HD); paraquat (early — limited value); thallium, bromide, atenolol/sotalol, gabapentin in renal failure, acetaminophen (massive with acidosis, when NAC not available); dialysis is not useful for highly protein-bound or lipid-soluble drugs with large Vd (TCAs, digoxin, benzodiazepines, opioids, OP compounds, phenothiazines, calcium channel blockers, β-blockers like propranolol); complications — hypotension, bleeding (heparin), infection, line complications, disequilibrium syndrome; (B) haemoperfusion (HP) — blood is passed through a cartridge containing adsorbent material (activated charcoal coated with polymer, or resin — Amberlite XAD-4) which directly adsorbs toxins; effective for lipid-soluble and protein-bound substances not removed by dialysis (since adsorption competes with protein binding); indications — theophylline (classic indication), phenobarbitone and other barbiturates, carbamazepine, phenytoin, meprobamate, glutethimide, paraquat (early, within hours — controversial), Amanita phalloides toxins (amatoxins), chloramphenicol, disopyramide, some OP (limited evidence); complications — thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, hypocalcaemia, hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, bleeding (heparinisation), cartridge saturation (replace every 2–4 h), charcoal embolisation (rare); availability of cartridges limited; high-flux HD has largely replaced HP; (C) haemofiltration/continuous renal replacement therapy (CVVH, CVVHDF) — convective removal, slower, for haemodynamically unstable patients; useful for lithium (rebound prevention), metformin, larger molecules (up to 50,000 Da — e.g., aminoglycosides, vancomycin); (D) peritoneal dialysis — much less efficient (about 1/8th of HD), used only when HD is unavailable or in small children; (E) exchange transfusion — replacing patient's blood with donor blood; used in neonates and infants, severe methaemoglobinaemia unresponsive to methylene blue (or G6PD deficiency), severe haemolysis (arsine, chlorates), hyperbilirubinaemia, theophylline/salicylate in neonates; (F) plasmapheresis/plasma exchange — removes plasma (with protein-bound toxins) — highly protein-bound poisons with small Vd: Amanita mushroom toxins, thyroxine overdose, cisplatin, snake bite (controversial); (G) MARS (albumin dialysis) — liver failure, highly protein-bound drugs; (H) multiple-dose activated charcoal ('gut dialysis') — repeated oral charcoal (25–50 g every 4 h or 12.5 g/h) creates a sink in the gut lumen that draws drug from the blood across the intestinal mucosa (enteroenteric circulation) and interrupts enterohepatic recirculation; recommended (AACT) for life-threatening ingestion of carbamazepine, dapsone, phenobarbitone, quinine and theophylline; may be useful for digitoxin, phenytoin, salicylates (sustained release), oleander (yellow oleander — Sri Lankan trial mixed results); contraindicated with ileus, unprotected airway; complications — constipation, bezoar, aspiration. (III) Chelation (for metals — see antidotes) and cholestyramine (binds digitoxin, chlordecone in gut — enterohepatic interruption). (IV) Oxygen — enhanced pulmonary elimination of CO. Summary indications for enhanced elimination: severe poisoning with a poison whose elimination can be significantly increased (as above), rising/high plasma levels, impaired natural elimination (renal or hepatic failure), deterioration despite optimal supportive care, prolonged coma with complications, and when the benefit outweighs the risks; serial drug levels guide therapy; rebound redistribution after cessation (lithium, theophylline) requires monitoring.",
[["Urinary pH manipulation",[["Method","Agent","Target poisons","Status"],["Alkalinisation (pH 7.5–8.5)","IV NaHCO₃ + K⁺","Salicylates, phenobarbitone, chlorpropamide, 2,4-D, methotrexate","Recommended"],["Acid diuresis","NH₄Cl, ascorbic acid","Amphetamines, quinine, PCP","Abandoned (rhabdomyolysis, renal failure)"],["Forced neutral diuresis","Fluids + diuretics","—","Abandoned (fluid overload)"]]],
["Haemodialysis vs haemoperfusion",[["Feature","Haemodialysis","Haemoperfusion"],["Principle","Diffusion across membrane","Adsorption on charcoal/resin"],["Best for","Small, water-soluble, low protein-bound, small Vd","Lipid-soluble, protein-bound"],["Indications","Methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium, salicylates, metformin, valproate, theophylline","Theophylline, barbiturates, carbamazepine, paraquat, Amanita"],["Extra benefit","Corrects acidosis, electrolytes, renal failure","No metabolic correction"],["Complications","Hypotension, bleeding, disequilibrium","Thrombocytopenia, hypocalcaemia, hypoglycaemia"]]],
["Properties favouring dialysis",["Molecular weight < 500 Da","Water-soluble","Protein binding < 80%","Volume of distribution < 1 L/kg","Low endogenous clearance"]],
["Not removed by dialysis",["TCAs, digoxin","Benzodiazepines, opioids","OP compounds","Phenothiazines","Calcium channel blockers"]],
["Other methods",[["Method","Use"],["MDAC (gut dialysis)","Carbamazepine, dapsone, phenobarbitone, quinine, theophylline"],["CRRT","Unstable patients; lithium rebound"],["Peritoneal dialysis","When HD unavailable (low efficiency)"],["Exchange transfusion","Neonates, severe methaemoglobinaemia, haemolysis"],["Plasmapheresis","Highly protein-bound (Amanita, thyroxine)"],["Oxygen/HBO","Carbon monoxide"]]]],
{c:"Enhanced elimination removes absorbed poison by urinary alkalinisation for weak acids like salicylates and phenobarbitone (acid and neutral forced diuresis are abandoned), multiple-dose activated charcoal, haemodialysis for small water-soluble poisons such as methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium and salicylates, haemoperfusion for lipid-soluble protein-bound drugs like theophylline and barbiturates, and exchange transfusion or plasmapheresis in special cases, reserved for severe poisoning where benefit outweighs risk."});
